    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "3.3 Average and Instantaneous Acceleration"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "LEARNING OBJECTIVES"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "By the end of this section, you will be able to:"
    },
    {
      "type": "list",
      "text": "• Calculate the average acceleration between two points in time."
    },
    {
      "type": "list",
      "text": "• Calculate the instantaneous acceleration given the functional form of velocity."
    },
    {
      "type": "list",
      "text": "• Explain the vector nature of instantaneous acceleration and velocity."
    },
    {
      "type": "list",
      "text": "• Explain the difference between average acceleration and instantaneous acceleration."
    },
    {
      "type": "list",
      "text": "• Find instantaneous acceleration at a specified time on a graph of velocity versus time."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "The importance of understanding acceleration spans our day-to-day experience, as well as the vast reaches of outer space and the tiny world of subatomic physics. In everyday conversation, to accelerate means to speed up; applying the brake pedal causes a vehicle to slow down. We are familiar with the acceleration of our car, for example. The greater the acceleration, the greater the change in velocity over a given time. Acceleration is widely seen in experimental physics. In linear particle accelerator experiments, for example, subatomic particles are accelerated to very high velocities in collision experiments, which tell us information about the structure of the subatomic world"
    }
  ]
}

{
  "blocks": [
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "using the power rule to be:"
    },
    {
      "type": "equation",
      "text": "\\frac{d(At^n)}{dt} = Ant^{n-1}",
      "label": "3.7"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Note that if there are additional terms added together, this power rule of differentiation can be done multiple times and the solution is the sum of those terms. The following example illustrates the use of Equation 3.7."
    },
    {
      "type": "example",
      "text": "EXAMPLE 3.3\nInstantaneous Velocity Versus Average Velocity\nThe position of a particle is given by $x(t) = (3.0 \\text{ m/s})t + (0.50 \\text{ m/s}^3)t^3$.\na. Using Equation 3.4 and Equation 3.7, find the instantaneous velocity at $t = 2.0 \\text{ s}$.\nb. Calculate the average velocity between 1.0 s and 3.0 s.\nStrategy\nEquation 3.4 gives the instantaneous velocity of the particle as the derivative of the position function. Looking at the form of the position function given, we see that it is a polynomial in $t$. Therefore, we can use Equation 3.7, the power rule from calculus, to find the solution. We use Equation 3.6 to calculate the average velocity of the particle.\nSolution\na. $v(t) = \\frac{dx(t)}{dt} = (3.0 \\text{ m/s}) + (1.5 \\text{ m/s}^3)t^2$.\nSubstituting $t = 2.0 \\text{ s}$ into this equation gives $v(2.0 \\text{ s}) = (3.0 \\text{ m/s}) + (1.5 \\text{ m/s}^3)(2.0 \\text{ s})^2 = 9.0 \\text{ m/s}$.\nb. To determine the average velocity of the particle between 1.0 s and 3.0 s, we calculate the values of $x(1.0 \\text{ s})$ and $x(3.0 \\text{ s})$:\n$x(1.0 \\text{ s}) = [(3.0 \\text{ m/s})(1.0 \\text{ s}) + (0.50 \\text{ m/s}^3)(1.0 \\text{ s})^3] = 3.5 \\text{ m}$\n$x(3.0 \\text{ s}) = [(3.0 \\text{ m/s})(3.0 \\text{ s}) + (0.50 \\text{ m/s}^3)(3.0 \\text{ s})^3] = 22.5 \\text{ m}$\nThen the average velocity is\n$\\bar{v} = \\frac{x(3.0 \\text{ s}) - x(1.0 \\text{ s})}{(3.0 \\text{ s}) - (1.0 \\text{ s})} = \\frac{(22.5 - 3.5) \\text{ m}}{(3.0 - 1.0) \\text{ s}} = 9.5 \\text{ m/s}$.\nSignificance\nIn the limit that the time interval used to calculate $\\bar{v}$ goes to zero, the value obtained for $\\bar{v}$ converges to the value of $v$. The value of $9.0 \\text{ m/s}$ that we calculated in part a for the instantaneous value at $t = 2.0 \\text{ s}$ is close to the average value of $9.5 \\text{ m/s}$ found in b for a short interval that includes $t = 2.0 \\text{ s}$. If we were to make the interval shorter, the values in the calculation of the average would become even closer."
    },
    {
      "type": "example",
      "text": "EXAMPLE 3.4\nInstantaneous Velocity Versus Speed\nConsider the motion of a particle in which the position is $x(t) = (3.0 \\text{ m/s})t - (3 \\text{ m/s}^2)t^2$.\na. What is the instantaneous velocity at $t = 0.25 \\text{ s}$, $t = 0.50 \\text{ s}$, and $t = 1.0 \\text{ s}$?\nb. What is the speed of the particle at these times?\nStrategy\nThe instantaneous velocity is the derivative of the position function and the speed is the magnitude of the instantaneous velocity. We use Equation 3.4 and Equation 3.7 to solve for instantaneous velocity."
    }
  ]
}

{
