    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "If an object in motion has a velocity in the positive direction with respect to a chosen origin and it acquires a constant negative acceleration, the object eventually comes to a rest and reverses direction. If we wait long enough, the object passes through the origin going in the opposite direction. This is illustrated in Figure 3.11."
    },
    {
      "type": "figure",
      "text": "FIGURE 3.11 An object in motion with a velocity vector toward the east under negative acceleration comes to a rest and reverses direction. It passes the origin going in the opposite direction after a long enough time. — A motion diagram showing a horizontal axis with an Origin in the center, West to the left, and East to the right. Two positions of a black dot are shown: one at $v_i$ with a green velocity vector pointing left (West), and one at $v_o$ with a green velocity vector pointing right (East). Below the axis, a purple acceleration vector $a$ points left (West).",
