{
  "blocks": [
    {
      "type": "list",
      "text": "b. Find the instantaneous velocity at $t = 1$, $2$, $3$, and $5\\text{ s}$.\nc. Find the instantaneous acceleration at $t = 1$, $2$, $3$, and $5\\text{ s}$.\nd. Interpret the results of (c) in terms of the directions of the acceleration and velocity vectors."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Strategy"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "We find the functional form of acceleration by taking the derivative of the velocity function. Then, we calculate the values of instantaneous velocity and acceleration from the given functions for each. For part (d), we need to compare the directions of velocity and acceleration at each time."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Solution"
    },
    {
      "type": "list",
      "text": "a. $a(t) = \\frac{dv(t)}{dt} = (20\\text{ m/s}^2) - (10\\text{ m/s}^3)t$\nb. $v(1\\text{ s}) = 15\\text{ m/s}$, $v(2\\text{ s}) = 20\\text{ m/s}$, $v(3\\text{ s}) = 15\\text{ m/s}$, $v(5\\text{ s}) = -25\\text{ m/s}$\nc. $a(1\\text{ s}) = 10\\text{ m/s}^2$, $a(2\\text{ s}) = 0\\text{ m/s}^2$, $a(3\\text{ s}) = -10\\text{ m/s}^2$, $a(5\\text{ s}) = -30\\text{ m/s}^2$\nd. At $t = 1\\text{ s}$, velocity is positive and acceleration is positive, so both velocity and acceleration are in the same direction. The particle is moving faster."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "At $t = 2\\text{ s}$, velocity has increased to $v(2\\text{ s}) = 20\\text{ m/s}$, where it is maximum, which corresponds to the time when the acceleration is zero. We see that the maximum velocity occurs when the slope of the velocity function is zero, which is just the zero of the acceleration function."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "At $t = 3\\text{ s}$, velocity is $v(3\\text{ s}) = 15\\text{ m/s}$ and acceleration is negative. The particle has reduced its velocity and the acceleration vector is negative. The particle is slowing down."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "At $t = 5\\text{ s}$, velocity is $v(5\\text{ s}) = -25\\text{ m/s}$ and acceleration is increasingly negative. Between the times $t = 3\\text{ s}$ and $t = 5\\text{ s}$ the particle has decreased its velocity to zero and then become negative, thus reversing its direction. The particle is now speeding up again, but in the opposite direction."
