{
  "blocks": [
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "as well as the origin of the universe. In space, cosmic rays are subatomic particles that have been accelerated to very high energies in supernovas (exploding massive stars) and active galactic nuclei. It is important to understand the processes that accelerate cosmic rays because these rays contain highly penetrating radiation that can damage electronics flown on spacecraft, for example."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Average Acceleration"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "The formal definition of acceleration is consistent with these notions just described, but is more inclusive."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "AVERAGE ACCELERATION"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Average acceleration is the rate at which velocity changes:"
    },
    {
      "type": "equation",
      "text": "\\bar{a} = \\frac{\\Delta v}{\\Delta t} = \\frac{v_f - v_0}{t_f - t_0}",
      "label": "3.8"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "where $\\bar{a}$ is average acceleration, $v$ is velocity, and $t$ is time. (The bar over the a means average acceleration.)"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Because acceleration is velocity in meters per second divided by time in seconds, the SI units for acceleration are often abbreviated m/s$^2$—that is, meters per second squared or meters per second per second. This literally means by how many meters per second the velocity changes every second. Recall that velocity is a vector—it has both magnitude and direction—which means that a change in velocity can be a change in magnitude (or speed), but it can also be a change in direction. For example, if a runner traveling at 10 km/h due east slows to a stop, reverses direction, and continues her run at 10 km/h due west, her velocity has changed as a result of the change in direction, although the magnitude of the velocity is the same in both directions. Thus, acceleration occurs when velocity changes in magnitude (an increase or decrease in speed) or in direction, or both."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "ACCELERATION AS A VECTOR"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Acceleration is a vector in the same direction as the change in velocity, $\\Delta v$. Since velocity is a vector, it can change in magnitude or in direction, or both. Acceleration is, therefore, a change in speed or direction, or both."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Keep in mind that although acceleration is in the direction of the change in velocity, it is not always in the direction of motion. When an object slows down, its acceleration is opposite to the direction of its motion. Although this is commonly referred to as deceleration Figure 3.10, we say the train is accelerating in a direction opposite to its direction of motion."
    },
    {
      "type": "figure",
      "text": "FIGURE 3.10 A subway train in Sao Paulo, Brazil, accelerates opposite to the motion as it comes into a station. It is accelerating in a direction opposite to its direction of motion. (credit: modification of work by Yusuke Kawasaki) — A photograph of a subway train arriving at a station. A green arrow labeled v points in the direction of the train's motion, and a purple arrow labeled a points in the opposite direction.",
      "label": "Figure 3.10",
      "box": [635, 318, 833, 681]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "The term deceleration can cause confusion in our analysis because it is not a vector and it does not point to a specific direction with respect to a coordinate system, so we do not use it. Acceleration is a vector, so we must choose the appropriate sign for it in our chosen coordinate system. In the case of the train in Figure 3.10,"
    }
  ]
}

{
  "blocks": [
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Getting a Feel for Acceleration"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "You are probably used to experiencing acceleration when you step into an elevator, or step on the gas pedal in your car. However, acceleration is happening to many other objects in our universe with which we don't have direct contact. Table 3.2 presents the acceleration of various objects. We can see the magnitudes of the accelerations extend over many orders of magnitude."
    },
    {
      "type": "table",
      "text": "| Acceleration | Value ($\\text{m/s}^2$) |\n| --- | --- |\n| High-speed train | 0.25 |\n| Elevator | 2 |\n| Cheetah | 5 |\n| Object in a free fall without air resistance near the surface of Earth | 9.8 |\n| Space shuttle maximum during launch | 29 |\n| Parachutist peak during normal opening of parachute | 59 |\n| F16 aircraft pulling out of a dive | 79 |\n| Explosive seat ejection from aircraft | 147 |\n| Sprint missile | 982 |\n| Fastest rocket sled peak acceleration | 1540 |\n| Jumping flea | 3200 |\n| Baseball struck by a bat | 30,000 |\n| Closing jaws of a trap-jaw ant | 1,000,000 |\n| Proton in the large Hadron collider | $1.9 \\times 10^9$ |\n\nTABLE 3.2 Typical Values of Acceleration (credit: Wikipedia: Orders of Magnitude (acceleration))"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "In this table, we see that typical accelerations vary widely with different objects and have nothing to do with object size or how massive it is. Acceleration can also vary widely with time during the motion of an object. A drag racer has a large acceleration just after its start, but then it tapers off as the vehicle reaches a constant velocity. Its average acceleration can be quite different from its instantaneous acceleration at a particular time during its motion. Figure 3.17 compares graphically average acceleration with instantaneous acceleration for two very different motions."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "blocks": [
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Solution"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "First, identify the knowns: $v_0 = 0$, $v_f = -15.0 \\text{ m/s}$ (the negative sign indicates direction toward the west), $\\Delta t = 1.80 \\text{ s}$."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Second, find the change in velocity. Since the horse is going from zero to $-15.0 \\text{ m/s}$, its change in velocity equals its final velocity:"
    },
    {
      "type": "equation",
      "text": "\\Delta v = v_f - v_0 = v_f = -15.0 \\text{ m/s}."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Last, substitute the known values ($\\Delta v$ and $\\Delta t$) and solve for the unknown $\\bar{a}$:"
    },
    {
      "type": "equation",
      "text": "\\bar{a} = \\frac{\\Delta v}{\\Delta t} = \\frac{-15.0 \\text{ m/s}}{1.80 \\text{ s}} = -8.33 \\text{ m/s}^2."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Significance"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "The negative sign for acceleration indicates that acceleration is toward the west. An acceleration of $8.33 \\text{ m/s}^2$ due west means the horse increases its velocity by $8.33 \\text{ m/s}$ due west each second; that is, 8.33 meters per second per second, which we write as $8.33 \\text{ m/s}^2$. This is truly an average acceleration, because the ride is not smooth. We see later that an acceleration of this magnitude would require the rider to hang on with a force nearly equal to his weight."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "CHECK YOUR UNDERSTANDING 3.3"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Protons in a linear accelerator are accelerated from rest to $2.0 \\times 10^7 \\text{ m/s}$ in $10^{-4} \\text{ s}$. What is the average acceleration of the protons?"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Instantaneous Acceleration"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Instantaneous acceleration $a$, or acceleration at a specific instant in time, is obtained using the same process discussed for instantaneous velocity. That is, we calculate the average acceleration between two points in time separated by $\\Delta t$ and let $\\Delta t$ approach zero. The result is the derivative of the velocity function $v(t)$, which is instantaneous acceleration and is expressed mathematically as"
    },
    {
      "type": "equation",
      "text": "a(t) = \\frac{d}{dt}v(t).",
      "label": "3.9"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Thus, similar to velocity being the derivative of the position function, instantaneous acceleration is the derivative of the velocity function. We can show this graphically in the same way as instantaneous velocity. In Figure 3.14, instantaneous acceleration at time $t_0$ is the slope of the tangent line to the velocity-versus-time graph at time $t_0$. We see that average acceleration $\\bar{a} = \\frac{\\Delta v}{\\Delta t}$ approaches instantaneous acceleration as $\\Delta t$ approaches zero. Also in part (a) of the figure, we see that velocity has a maximum when its slope is zero. This time corresponds to the zero of the acceleration function. In part (b), instantaneous acceleration at the minimum velocity is shown, which is also zero, since the slope of the curve is zero there, too. Thus, for a given velocity function, the zeros of the acceleration function give either the minimum or the maximum velocity."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "blocks": [
    {
      "type": "figure",
      "text": "FIGURE 3.19 The airplane lands with an initial velocity of $70.0\\text{ m/s}$ and slows to a final velocity of $10.0\\text{ m/s}$ before heading for the terminal. Note the acceleration is negative because its direction is opposite to its velocity, which is positive. — Two diagrams showing an airplane on a horizontal line representing motion. In the left diagram, at $t_0 = 0$, a red airplane points right with velocity vector $\\bar{v}_0 = 70.0\\text{ m/s}$ pointing right, and acceleration vector $\\bar{a} = -1.50\\text{ m/s}^2$ pointing left. In the right diagram, at $t = 40.0\\text{ s}$, the airplane points right with velocity vector $\\bar{v} = 10.0\\text{ m/s}$ pointing right, and acceleration vector $\\bar{a} = -1.50\\text{ m/s}^2$ pointing left.",
      "label": "Figure 3.19",
      "box": [68, 153, 175, 849]
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Significance"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "The final velocity is much less than the initial velocity, as desired when slowing down, but is still positive (see figure). With jet engines, reverse thrust can be maintained long enough to stop the plane and start moving it backward, which is indicated by a negative final velocity, but is not the case here."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "In addition to being useful in problem solving, the equation $v = v_0 + at$ gives us insight into the relationships among velocity, acceleration, and time. We can see, for example, that"
    },
    {
      "type": "list",
      "text": "Final velocity depends on how large the acceleration is and how long it lasts"
    },
    {
      "type": "list",
      "text": "If the acceleration is zero, then the final velocity equals the initial velocity ($v = v_0$), as expected (in other words, velocity is constant)"
    },
    {
      "type": "list",
      "text": "If $a$ is negative, then the final velocity is less than the initial velocity"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "All these observations fit our intuition. Note that it is always useful to examine basic equations in light of our intuition and experience to check that they do indeed describe nature accurately."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Solving for Final Position with Constant Acceleration"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "We can combine the previous equations to find a third equation that allows us to calculate the final position of an object experiencing constant acceleration. We start with"
    },
    {
      "type": "equation",
      "text": "v = v_0 + at."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Adding $v_0$ to each side of this equation and dividing by 2 gives"
    },
    {
      "type": "equation",
      "text": "\\frac{v_0 + v}{2} = v_0 + \\frac{1}{2}at."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Since $\\frac{v_0+v}{2} = \\bar{v}$ for constant acceleration, we have"
    },
    {
      "type": "equation",
      "text": "\\bar{v} = v_0 + \\frac{1}{2}at."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Now we substitute this expression for $\\bar{v}$ into the equation for displacement, $x = x_0 + \\bar{v}t$, yielding"
    },
    {
      "type": "equation",
      "text": "x = x_0 + v_0t + \\frac{1}{2}at^2 \\quad (\\text{constant } a).",
      "label": "3.13"
    },
    {
      "type": "example",
      "text": "EXAMPLE 3.8\nCalculating Displacement of an Accelerating Object\nDragsters can achieve an average acceleration of $26.0\\text{ m/s}^2$. Suppose a dragster accelerates from rest at this rate for $5.56\\text{ s}$ (Figure 3.20). How far does it travel in this time?"
    }
  ]
}

{
  "blocks": [
    {
      "type": "figure",
      "text": "FIGURE 3.18 (a) Velocity-versus-time graph with constant acceleration showing the initial and final velocities v_0 and v. The average velocity is \\frac{1}{2}(v_0 + v) = 60 \\text{ km/h}. (b) Velocity-versus-time graph with an acceleration that changes with time. The average velocity is not given by \\frac{1}{2}(v_0 + v), but is greater than 60 km/h. — Two line graphs with Velocity (km/h) on the vertical axis (from 0 to 100 with tick marks every 10) and Time (h) on the horizontal axis (from 0 to 3 with tick marks every 0.5). Graph (a) shows a straight line increasing from (1, 40) labeled v_0 to (2, 80) labeled v, representing constant acceleration. Graph (b) shows a curved line starting at (1, 40) labeled v_0 and curving upwards to (2, 80) labeled v, representing changing acceleration.",
      "label": "FIGURE 3.18",
      "box": [68, 128, 329, 871]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "FIGURE 3.18 (a) Velocity-versus-time graph with constant acceleration showing the initial and final velocities $v_0$ and $v$. The average velocity is $\\frac{1}{2}(v_0 + v) = 60 \\text{ km/h}$. (b) Velocity-versus-time graph with an acceleration that changes with time. The average velocity is not given by $\\frac{1}{2}(v_0 + v)$, but is greater than 60 km/h."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Solving for Final Velocity from Acceleration and Time"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "We can derive another useful equation by manipulating the definition of acceleration:"
    },
    {
      "type": "equation",
      "text": "a = \\frac{\\Delta v}{\\Delta t}"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Substituting the simplified notation for $\\Delta v$ and $\\Delta t$ gives us"
    },
    {
      "type": "equation",
      "text": "a = \\frac{v - v_0}{t} \\quad (\\text{constant } a)"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Solving for $v$ yields"
    },
    {
      "type": "equation",
      "text": "v = v_0 + at \\quad (\\text{constant } a)",
      "label": "3.12"
    },
    {
      "type": "example",
      "text": "EXAMPLE 3.7\nCalculating Final Velocity\nAn airplane lands with an initial velocity of $70.0\\text{ m/s}$ and then accelerates opposite to the motion at $1.50\\text{ m/s}^2$ for $40.0\\text{ s}$. What is its final velocity?\nStrategy\nFirst, we identify the knowns: $v_0 = 70\\text{ m/s}$, $a = -1.50\\text{ m/s}^2$, $t = 40\\text{ s}$.\nSecond, we identify the unknown; in this case, it is final velocity $v_f$.\nLast, we determine which equation to use. To do this we figure out which kinematic equation gives the unknown in terms of the knowns. We calculate the final velocity using Equation 3.12, $v = v_0 + at$.\nSolution\nSubstitute the known values and solve:\n$$v = v_0 + at = 70.0\\text{ m/s} + \\left(-1.50\\text{ m/s}^2\\right) (40.0\\text{ s}) = 10.0\\text{ m/s}.$$"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Figure 3.19 is a sketch that shows the acceleration and velocity vectors."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "blocks": [
    {
      "type": "figure",
      "text": "FIGURE 3.16 (a) Velocity versus time. Tangent lines are indicated at times 1, 2, and 3 s. The slopes of the tangent lines are the accelerations. At t = 3 s, velocity is positive. At t = 5 s, velocity is negative, indicating the particle has reversed direction. (b) Acceleration versus time. Comparing the values of accelerations given by the black dots with the corresponding slopes of the tangent lines (slopes of lines through black dots) in (a), we see they are identical. — Two graphs showing motion over time. (a) Velocity v(t) in m/s vs Time (s). A parabolic curve opening downwards starting at (0,0) and ending at (5,-25). Tangent lines are drawn at t=1s, t=2s, and t=3s. (b) Acceleration a(t) in m/s^2 vs Time (s). A straight line with negative slope starting at (0,20) and ending at (5,-30). Black dots are plotted at t=1s, t=2s, and t=3s.",
      "label": "FIGURE 3.16",
      "box": [
        68,
        350,
        658,
        665
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Significance"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "By doing both a numerical and graphical analysis of velocity and acceleration of the particle, we can learn much about its motion. The numerical analysis complements the graphical analysis in giving a total view of the motion. The zero of the acceleration function corresponds to the maximum of the velocity in this example. Also in this example, when acceleration is positive and in the same direction as velocity, velocity increases. As acceleration tends toward zero, eventually becoming negative, the velocity reaches a maximum, after which it starts decreasing. If we wait long enough, velocity also becomes negative, indicating a reversal of direction. A real-world example of this type of motion is a car with a velocity that is increasing to a maximum, after which it starts slowing down, comes to a stop, then reverses direction."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "CHECK YOUR UNDERSTANDING 3.4"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "An airplane lands on a runway traveling east. Describe its acceleration."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "blocks": [
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Speed"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "In everyday language, most people use the terms speed and velocity interchangeably. In physics, however, they do not have the same meaning and are distinct concepts. One major difference is that speed has no direction; that is, speed is a scalar."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "We can calculate the average speed by finding the total distance traveled divided by the elapsed time:"
    },
    {
      "type": "equation",
      "text": "\\text{Average speed} = \\bar{s} = \\frac{\\text{Total distance}}{\\text{Elapsed time}}",
      "label": "3.5"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Average speed is not necessarily the same as the magnitude of the average velocity, which is found by dividing the magnitude of the total displacement by the elapsed time. For example, if a trip starts and ends at the same location, the total displacement is zero, and therefore the average velocity is zero. The average speed, however, is not zero, because the total distance traveled is greater than zero. If we take a road trip of 300 km and need to be at our destination at a certain time, then we would be interested in our average speed."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "However, we can calculate the instantaneous speed from the magnitude of the instantaneous velocity:"
    },
    {
      "type": "equation",
      "text": "\\text{Instantaneous speed} = |v(t)|.",
      "label": "3.6"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "If a particle is moving along the x-axis at +7.0 m/s and another particle is moving along the same axis at −7.0 m/s, they have different velocities, but both have the same speed of 7.0 m/s. Some typical speeds are shown in the following table."
    },
    {
      "type": "table",
      "text": "| Speed | m/s | mi/h |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| Continental drift | $10^{-9}$ | $2 \\times 10^{-9}$ |\n| Brisk walk | 1.7 | 3.9 |\n| Cyclist | 4.4 | 10 |\n| Sprint runner | 12.2 | 27 |\n| Rural speed limit | 24.6 | 56 |\n| Official land speed record | 341.1 | 763 |\n| Speed of sound at sea level | 343 | 768 |\n| Space shuttle on reentry | 7800 | 17,500 |\n| Escape velocity of Earth* | 11,200 | 25,000 |\n| Orbital speed of Earth around the Sun | 29,783 | 66,623 |\n| Speed of light in a vacuum | 299,792,458 | 670,616,629 |",
      "label": "TABLE 3.1 Speeds of Various Objects *Escape velocity is the velocity at which an object must be launched so that it overcomes Earth’s gravity and is not pulled back toward Earth."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Calculating Instantaneous Velocity"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "When calculating instantaneous velocity, we need to specify the explicit form of the position function $x(t)$. If each term in the $x(t)$ equation has the form of $At^n$ where $A$ is a constant and $n$ is an integer, this can be differentiated"
    }
  ]
}

{
  "blocks": [
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "but top-notch dragsters can do a quarter mile in even less time than this. If the dragster were given an initial velocity, this would add another term to the distance equation. If the same acceleration and time are used in the equation, the distance covered would be much greater."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "What else can we learn by examining the equation $x = x_0 + v_0 t + \\frac{1}{2}at^2$? We can see the following relationships:"
    },
    {
      "type": "list",
      "text": "• Displacement depends on the square of the elapsed time when acceleration is not zero. In Example 3.8, the dragster covers only one-fourth of the total distance in the first half of the elapsed time.\n• If acceleration is zero, then initial velocity equals average velocity ($\\bar{v} = v_0$), and $x = x_0 + v_0 t + \\frac{1}{2}at^2$ becomes $x = x_0 + v_0 t$."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Solving for Final Velocity from Distance and Acceleration"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "A fourth useful equation can be obtained from another algebraic manipulation of previous equations. If we solve $v = v_0 + at$ for t, we get"
    },
    {
      "type": "equation",
      "text": "t = \\frac{v - v_0}{a}"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Substituting this and $\\bar{v} = \\frac{v_0 + v}{2}$ into $x = x_0 + \\bar{v}t$, we get"
    },
    {
      "type": "equation",
      "text": "v^2 = v_0^2 + 2a(x - x_0)",
      "label": "3.14"
    },
    {
      "type": "example",
      "text": "EXAMPLE 3.9\nCalculating Final Velocity\nCalculate the final velocity of the dragster in Example 3.8 without using information about time.\nStrategy\nThe equation $v^2 = v_0^2 + 2a(x - x_0)$ is ideally suited to this task because it relates velocities, acceleration, and displacement, and no time information is required.\nSolution\nFirst, we identify the known values. We know that $v_0 = 0$, since the dragster starts from rest. We also know that $x - x_0 = 402\\text{ m}$ (this was the answer in Example 3.8). The average acceleration was given by $a = 26.0\\text{ m/s}^2$.\nSecond, we substitute the knowns into the equation $v^2 = v_0^2 + 2a(x - x_0)$ and solve for v:\nv^2 = 0 + 2(26.0\\text{ m/s}^2)(402\\text{ m}).\nThus,\nv^2 = 2.09 \\times 10^4\\text{ m}^2/\\text{s}^2\nv = \\sqrt{2.09 \\times 10^4\\text{ m}^2/\\text{s}^2} = 145\\text{ m/s}.\nSignificance\nA velocity of 145 m/s is about 522 km/h, or about 324 mi/h, but even this breakneck speed is short of the record for the quarter mile. Also, note that a square root has two values; we took the positive value to indicate a velocity in the same direction as the acceleration."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "An examination of the equation $v^2 = v_0^2 + 2a(x - x_0)$ can produce additional insights into the general relationships among physical quantities:"
    },
    {
      "type": "list",
      "text": "• The final velocity depends on how large the acceleration is and the distance over which it acts."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "blocks": [
    {
      "type": "figure",
      "text": "FIGURE 3.22 Sample sketch to visualize acceleration opposite to the motion and stopping distance of a car. — A red car is shown moving to the right with initial velocity $v_0 = 30.0\\text{ m/s}$ towards a traffic light showing a red signal and final velocity $v_f = 0\\text{ m/s}$. Displacement is denoted by $\\Delta x = ?$. Acceleration vectors pointing to the left are shown as $a_{\\text{dry}} = -7.00\\text{ m/s}^2$ and $a_{\\text{wet}} = -5.00\\text{ m/s}^2$. Students should use this sketch to identify initial conditions and accelerations for stopping distance calculations.",
      "label": "Figure 3.22",
      "box": [68, 203, 239, 796]
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Solution"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "a. First, we need to identify the knowns and what we want to solve for. We know that $v_0 = 30.0\\text{ m/s}$, $v = 0$, and $a = -7.00\\text{ m/s}^2$ ($a$ is negative because it is in a direction opposite to velocity). We take $x_0$ to be zero. We are looking for displacement $\\Delta x$, or $x - x_0$."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Second, we identify the equation that will help us solve the problem. The best equation to use is"
    },
    {
      "type": "equation",
      "text": "v^2 = v_0^2 + 2a(x - x_0)."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "This equation is best because it includes only one unknown, $x$. We know the values of all the other variables in this equation. (Other equations would allow us to solve for $x$, but they require us to know the stopping time, $t$, which we do not know. We could use them, but it would entail additional calculations.)"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Third, we rearrange the equation to solve for $x$:"
    },
    {
      "type": "equation",
      "text": "x - x_0 = \\frac{v^2 - v_0^2}{2a}"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "and substitute the known values:"
    },
    {
      "type": "equation",
      "text": "x - 0 = \\frac{0^2 - (30.0\\text{ m/s})^2}{2(-7.00\\text{ m/s}^2)}."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Thus,"
    },
    {
      "type": "equation",
      "text": "x = 64.3\\text{ m on dry concrete.}"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "b. This part can be solved in exactly the same manner as (a). The only difference is that the acceleration is $-5.00\\text{ m/s}^2$. The result is"
    },
    {
      "type": "equation",
      "text": "x_{\\text{wet}} = 90.0\\text{ m on wet concrete.}"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "c. When the driver reacts, the stopping distance is the same as it is in (a) and (b) for dry and wet concrete. So, to answer this question, we need to calculate how far the car travels during the reaction time, and then add that to the stopping time. It is reasonable to assume the velocity remains constant during the driver's reaction time."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "To do this, we, again, identify the knowns and what we want to solve for. We know that $\\bar{v} = 30.0\\text{ m/s}$, $t_{\\text{reaction}} = 0.500\\text{ s}$, and $a_{\\text{reaction}} = 0$. We take $x_{\\text{0-reaction}}$ to be zero. We are looking for $x_{\\text{reaction}}$."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Second, as before, we identify the best equation to use. In this case, $x = x_0 + \\bar{v}t$ works well because the only unknown value is $x$, which is what we want to solve for."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Third, we substitute the knowns to solve the equation:"
    },
    {
      "type": "equation",
      "text": "x = 0 + (30.0\\text{ m/s})(0.500\\text{ s}) = 15.0\\text{ m}."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "This means the car travels $15.0\\text{ m}$ while the driver reacts, making the total displacements in the two cases of dry and wet concrete $15.0\\text{ m}$ greater than if he reacted instantly."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Last, we then add the displacement during the reaction time to the displacement when braking (Figure 3.23),"
    },
    {
      "type": "equation",
      "text": "x_{\\text{braking}} + x_{\\text{reaction}} = x_{\\text{total}},"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "and find (a) to be $64.3\\text{ m} + 15.0\\text{ m} = 79.3\\text{ m}$ when dry and (b) to be $90.0\\text{ m} + 15.0\\text{ m} = 105\\text{ m}$ when wet."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "blocks": [
    {
      "type": "figure",
      "text": "FIGURE 3.23 The distance necessary to stop a car varies greatly, depending on road conditions and driver reaction time. Shown here are the braking distances for dry and wet pavement, as calculated in this example, for a car traveling initially at 30.0 m/s. Also shown are the total distances traveled from the point when the driver first sees a light turn red, assuming a 0.500-s reaction time. — A horizontal axis representing Position x (m) from 0 to 100 with tick marks at 15, 50, and 100. Two main sections are illustrated: a yellow-shaded reaction zone of length 15 (representing 0.500-s reaction time), followed by braking distances. For dry pavement, the braking distance is 64.3 m and the total stopping distance is 79.3 m with a red car icon shown at each position. For wet pavement, the braking distance is 90.0 m and the total stopping distance is 105 m with a red car icon shown at each position.",
      "label": "FIGURE 3.23",
      "box": [42, 145, 259, 854]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "FIGURE 3.23 The distance necessary to stop a car varies greatly, depending on road conditions and driver reaction time. Shown here are the braking distances for dry and wet pavement, as calculated in this example, for a car traveling initially at 30.0 m/s. Also shown are the total distances traveled from the point when the driver first sees a light turn red, assuming a 0.500-s reaction time."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Significance"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "The displacements found in this example seem reasonable for stopping a fast-moving car. It should take longer to stop a car on wet pavement than dry. It is interesting that reaction time adds significantly to the displacements, but more important is the general approach to solving problems. We identify the knowns and the quantities to be determined, then find an appropriate equation. If there is more than one unknown, we need as many independent equations as there are unknowns to solve. There is often more than one way to solve a problem. The various parts of this example can, in fact, be solved by other methods, but the solutions presented here are the shortest."
    },
    {
      "type": "example",
      "text": "EXAMPLE 3.11\nCalculating Time\nSuppose a car merges into freeway traffic on a 200-m-long ramp. If its initial velocity is $10.0\\text{ m/s}$ and it accelerates at $2.00\\text{ m/s}^2$, how long does it take the car to travel the $200\\text{ m}$ up the ramp? (Such information might be useful to a traffic engineer.)"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Strategy"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "First, we draw a sketch Figure 3.24. We are asked to solve for time $t$. As before, we identify the known quantities to choose a convenient physical relationship (that is, an equation with one unknown, $t$.)"
    },
    {
      "type": "figure",
      "text": "FIGURE 3.24 Sketch of a car accelerating on a freeway ramp. — A blue car is shown on a horizontal road segment of length from x_0 = 0 to x = 200 m, with a time variable t = ? above the car. Initial conditions are x_0 = 0 and v_0 = 10.0 m/s, final position is x = 200 m and final velocity v = ?, and an acceleration vector points right with a = 2.00 m/s^2.",
      "label": "FIGURE 3.24",
      "box": [621, 233, 765, 761]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "FIGURE 3.24 Sketch of a car accelerating on a freeway ramp."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Solution"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Again, we identify the knowns and what we want to solve for. We know that $x_0 = 0$, $v_0 = 10\\text{ m/s}$, $a = 2.00\\text{ m/s}^2$, and $x = 200\\text{ m}$."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "We need to solve for $t$. The equation $x = x_0 + v_0 t + \\frac{1}{2}at^2$ works best because the only unknown in the equation is the variable $t$, for which we need to solve. From this insight we see that when we input the knowns into the equation, we end up with a quadratic equation."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "blocks": [
    {
      "type": "equation",
      "text": "v = v_0 + at = 7133.3\\text{ m/s} + (20.0\\text{ m/s}^2)(120.0\\text{ s}) = 9533.3\\text{ m/s}.",
      "box": [74, 282, 89, 716]
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Significance"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "There are six variables in displacement, time, velocity, and acceleration that describe motion in one dimension. The initial conditions of a given problem can be many combinations of these variables. Because of this diversity, solutions may not be as easy as simple substitutions into one of the equations. This example illustrates that solutions to kinematics may require solving two simultaneous kinematic equations."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "With the basics of kinematics established, we can go on to many other interesting examples and applications. In the process of developing kinematics, we have also glimpsed a general approach to problem solving that produces both correct answers and insights into physical relationships. The next level of complexity in our kinematics problems involves the motion of two interrelated bodies, called two-body pursuit problems."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Two-Body Pursuit Problems"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Up until this point we have looked at examples of motion involving a single body. Even for the problem with two cars and the stopping distances on wet and dry roads, we divided this problem into two separate problems to find the answers. In a two-body pursuit problem, the motions of the objects are coupled—meaning, the unknown we seek depends on the motion of both objects. To solve these problems we write the equations of motion for each object and then solve them simultaneously to find the unknown. This is illustrated in Figure 3.25."
    },
    {
      "type": "figure",
      "text": "FIGURE 3.25 A two-body pursuit scenario where car 2 has a constant velocity and car 1 is behind with a constant acceleration. Car 1 catches up with car 2 at a later time. — A diagram showing two cars on a road: a red car 1 labeled $v_1 \\neq \\text{constant}$ with an acceleration arrow pointing right labeled \"Car 1 accelerates toward Car 2\", and a blue car 2 labeled $v_2 = \\text{constant}$. A second scene to the right shows \"At a later time Car 1 catches Car 2\".",
      "label": "Figure 3.25",
      "box": [390, 219, 483, 775]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "The time and distance required for car 1 to catch car 2 depends on the initial distance car 1 is from car 2 as well as the velocities of both cars and the acceleration of car 1. The kinematic equations describing the motion of both cars must be solved to find these unknowns."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Consider the following example."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "EXAMPLE 3.13"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Cheetah Catching a Gazelle"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "A cheetah waits in hiding behind a bush. The cheetah spots a gazelle running past at $10\\text{ m/s}$. At the instant the gazelle passes the cheetah, the cheetah accelerates from rest at $4\\text{ m/s}^2$ to catch the gazelle. (a) How long does it take the cheetah to catch the gazelle? (b) What is the displacement of the gazelle and cheetah?"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Strategy"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "We use the set of equations for constant acceleration to solve this problem. Since there are two objects in motion, we have separate equations of motion describing each animal. But what links the equations is a common parameter that has the same value for each animal. If we look at the problem closely, it is clear the common parameter to each animal is their position $x$ at a later time $t$. Since they both start at $x_0 = 0$, their displacements are the same at a later time $t$, when the cheetah catches up with the gazelle. If we pick the equation of motion that solves for the displacement for each animal, we can then set the equations equal to each other and solve for the unknown, which is time."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Solution"
    },
    {
      "type": "list",
      "text": "a. Equation for the gazelle: The gazelle has a constant velocity, which is its average velocity, since it is not accelerating. Therefore, we use Equation 3.10 with $x_0 = 0$:"
    }
  ]
}

{
  "blocks": [
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "We need to rearrange the equation to solve for t, then substituting the knowns into the equation:"
    },
    {
      "type": "equation",
      "text": "200\\text{ m} = 0\\text{ m} + (10.0\\text{ m/s})t + \\frac{1}{2}(2.00\\text{ m/s}^2)t^2."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "We then simplify the equation. The units of meters cancel because they are in each term. We can get the units of seconds to cancel by taking t = t s, where t is the magnitude of time and s is the unit. Doing so leaves"
    },
    {
      "type": "equation",
      "text": "200 = 10t + t^2."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "We then use the quadratic formula to solve for t,"
    },
    {
      "type": "equation",
      "text": "t^2 + 10t - 200 = 0"
    },
    {
      "type": "equation",
      "text": "t = \\frac{-b \\pm \\sqrt{b^2 - 4ac}}{2a},"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "which yields two solutions: t = 10.0 and t = −20.0. A negative value for time is unreasonable, since it would mean the event happened 20 s before the motion began. We can discard that solution. Thus,"
    },
    {
      "type": "equation",
      "text": "t = 10.0\\text{ s}."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Significance"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Whenever an equation contains an unknown squared, there are two solutions. In some problems both solutions are meaningful; in others, only one solution is reasonable. The 10.0-s answer seems reasonable for a typical freeway on-ramp."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "CHECK YOUR UNDERSTANDING 3.5"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "A rocket accelerates at a rate of 20 m/s$^2$ during launch. How long does it take the rocket to reach a velocity of 400 m/s?"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "EXAMPLE 3.12"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Acceleration of a Spaceship"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "A spaceship has left Earth’s orbit and is on its way to the Moon. It accelerates at 20 m/s$^2$ for 2 min and covers a distance of 1000 km. What are the initial and final velocities of the spaceship?"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Strategy"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "We are asked to find the initial and final velocities of the spaceship. Looking at the kinematic equations, we see that one equation will not give the answer. We must use one kinematic equation to solve for one of the velocities and substitute it into another kinematic equation to get the second velocity. Thus, we solve two of the kinematic equations simultaneously."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Solution"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "First we solve for $v_0$ using $x = x_0 + v_0t + \\frac{1}{2}at^2$:"
    },
    {
      "type": "equation",
      "text": "x - x_0 = v_0t + \\frac{1}{2}at^2"
    },
    {
      "type": "equation",
      "text": "1.0 \\times 10^6\\text{ m} = v_0(120.0\\text{ s}) + \\frac{1}{2}(20.0\\text{ m/s}^2)(120.0\\text{ s})^2"
    },
    {
      "type": "equation",
      "text": "v_0 = 7133.3\\text{ m/s}."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Then we substitute $v_0$ into $v = v_0 + at$ to solve for the final velocity:"
    }
  ]
}

{
  "blocks": [
    {
      "type": "equation",
      "text": "x = x_0 + \\bar{v}t = \\bar{v}t."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Equation for the cheetah: The cheetah is accelerating from rest, so we use Equation 3.13 with $x_0 = 0$ and $v_0 = 0$:"
    },
    {
      "type": "equation",
      "text": "x = x_0 + v_0 t + \\frac{1}{2}at^2 = \\frac{1}{2}at^2."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Now we have an equation of motion for each animal with a common parameter, which can be eliminated to find the solution. In this case, we solve for $t$:"
    },
    {
      "type": "equation",
      "text": "x = \\bar{v}t = \\frac{1}{2}at^2 \\\\ t = \\frac{2\\bar{v}}{a}."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "The gazelle has a constant velocity of $10\\text{ m/s}$, which is its average velocity. The acceleration of the cheetah is $4\\text{ m/s}^2$. Evaluating $t$, the time for the cheetah to reach the gazelle, we have"
    },
    {
      "type": "equation",
      "text": "t = \\frac{2\\bar{v}}{a} = \\frac{2(10\\text{ m/s})}{4\\text{ m/s}^2} = 5\\text{ s}."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "b. To get the displacement, we use either the equation of motion for the cheetah or the gazelle, since they should both give the same answer.\\nDisplacement of the cheetah:"
    },
    {
      "type": "equation",
      "text": "x = \\frac{1}{2}at^2 = \\frac{1}{2}(4\\text{ m/s}^2)(5)^2 = 50\\text{ m}."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Displacement of the gazelle:"
    },
    {
      "type": "equation",
      "text": "x = \\bar{v}t = 10\\text{ m/s}(5) = 50\\text{ m}."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "We see that both displacements are equal, as expected."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Significance"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "It is important to analyze the motion of each object and to use the appropriate kinematic equations to describe the individual motion. It is also important to have a good visual perspective of the two-body pursuit problem to see the common parameter that links the motion of both objects."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "CHECK YOUR UNDERSTANDING 3.6"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "A bicycle has a constant velocity of $10\\text{ m/s}$. A person starts from rest and begins to run to catch up to the bicycle in $30\\text{ s}$ when the bicycle is at the same position as the person. What is the acceleration of the person?"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "3.5 Free Fall"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "LEARNING OBJECTIVES"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "By the end of this section, you will be able to:"
    },
    {
      "type": "list",
      "text": "• Use the kinematic equations with the variables y and g to analyze free-fall motion.\\n• Describe how the values of the position, velocity, and acceleration change during a free fall.\\n• Solve for the position, velocity, and acceleration as functions of time when an object is in a free fall."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "An interesting application of Equation 3.4 through Equation 3.14 is called free fall, which describes the motion of an object falling in a gravitational field, such as near the surface of Earth or other celestial objects of planetary size. Let's assume the body is falling in a straight line perpendicular to the surface, so its motion is one-dimensional. For example, we can estimate the depth of a vertical mine shaft by dropping a rock into it and listening for the rock to hit the bottom. But \"falling,\" in the context of free fall, does not necessarily imply the body is moving from a greater height to a lesser height. If a ball is thrown upward, the equations of free fall apply equally to its ascent as well as its descent."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "blocks": [
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "One-Dimensional Motion Involving Gravity"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "The best way to see the basic features of motion involving gravity is to start with the simplest situations and then progress toward more complex ones. So, we start by considering straight up-and-down motion with no air resistance or friction. These assumptions mean the velocity (if there is any) is vertical. If an object is dropped, we know the initial velocity is zero when in free fall. When the object has left contact with whatever held or threw it, the object is in free fall. When the object is thrown, it has the same initial speed in free fall as it did before it was released. When the object comes in contact with the ground or any other object, it is no longer in free fall and its acceleration of $g$ is no longer valid. Under these circumstances, the motion is one-dimensional and has constant acceleration of magnitude $g$. We represent vertical displacement with the symbol $y$."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "KINEMATIC EQUATIONS FOR OBJECTS IN FREE FALL"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "We assume here that acceleration equals $-g$ (with the positive direction upward)."
    },
    {
      "type": "equation",
      "text": "v = v_0 - gt",
      "label": "3.15"
    },
    {
      "type": "equation",
      "text": "y = y_0 + v_0t - \\frac{1}{2}gt^2",
      "label": "3.16"
    },
    {
      "type": "equation",
      "text": "v^2 = v_0^2 - 2g(y - y_0)",
      "label": "3.17"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "PROBLEM-SOLVING STRATEGY"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Free Fall"
    },
    {
      "type": "list",
      "text": "1. Decide on the sign of the acceleration of gravity. In Equation 3.15 through Equation 3.17, acceleration $g$ is negative, which says the positive direction is upward and the negative direction is downward. In some problems, it may be useful to have acceleration $g$ as positive, indicating the positive direction is downward."
    },
    {
      "type": "list",
      "text": "2. Draw a sketch of the problem. This helps visualize the physics involved."
    },
    {
      "type": "list",
      "text": "3. Record the knowns and unknowns from the problem description. This helps devise a strategy for selecting the appropriate equations to solve the problem."
    },
    {
      "type": "list",
      "text": "4. Decide which of Equation 3.15 through Equation 3.17 are to be used to solve for the unknowns."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "EXAMPLE 3.14"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Free Fall of a Ball"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Figure 3.27 shows the positions of a ball, at 1-s intervals, with an initial velocity of $4.9\\text{ m/s}$ downward, that is thrown from the top of a $98\\text{-m-high}$ building. (a) How much time elapses before the ball reaches the ground? (b) What is the velocity when it arrives at the ground?"
    }
  ]
}

{
  "blocks": [
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Gravity"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "The most remarkable and unexpected fact about falling objects is that if air resistance and friction are negligible, then in a given location all objects fall toward the center of Earth with the same constant acceleration, independent of their mass. This experimentally determined fact is unexpected because we are so accustomed to the effects of air resistance and friction that we expect light objects to fall slower than heavy ones. Until Galileo Galilei (1564–1642) proved otherwise, people believed that a heavier object has a greater acceleration in a free fall. We now know this is not the case. In the absence of air resistance, heavy objects arrive at the ground at the same time as lighter objects when dropped from the same height Figure 3.26."
    },
    {
      "type": "figure",
      "text": "FIGURE 3.26 A hammer and a feather fall with the same constant acceleration if air resistance is negligible. This is a general characteristic of gravity not unique to Earth, as astronaut David R. Scott demonstrated in 1971 on the Moon, where the acceleration from gravity is only 1.67 m/s^2 and there is no atmosphere. — The figure shows three panels: the first shows a feather and a hammer falling at different rates in air; the second shows them falling at the same rate in a vacuum; the third shows an astronaut on the Moon holding a hammer and a feather, with both objects falling at the same rate.",
      "label": "Figure 3.26",
      "box": [218, 117, 546, 882]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "In the real world, air resistance can cause a lighter object to fall slower than a heavier object of the same size. A tennis ball reaches the ground after a baseball dropped at the same time. (It might be difficult to observe the difference if the height is not large.) Air resistance opposes the motion of an object through the air, and friction between objects—such as between clothes and a laundry chute or between a stone and a pool into which it is dropped—also opposes motion between them."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "For the ideal situations of these first few chapters, an object falling without air resistance or friction is defined to be in free fall. The force of gravity causes objects to fall toward the center of Earth. The acceleration of free-falling objects is therefore called acceleration due to gravity. Acceleration due to gravity is constant, which means we can apply the kinematic equations to any falling object where air resistance and friction are negligible. This opens to us a broad class of interesting situations."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Acceleration due to gravity is so important that its magnitude is given its own symbol, $g$. It is constant at any given location on Earth and has the average value"
    },
    {
      "type": "equation",
      "text": "g = 9.81 m/s^2 (or 32.2 ft/s^2)"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Although $g$ varies from 9.78 m/s^2 to 9.83 m/s^2, depending on latitude, altitude, underlying geological formations, and local topography, let’s use an average value of 9.8 m/s^2 rounded to two significant figures in this text unless specified otherwise. Neglecting these effects on the value of $g$ as a result of position on Earth’s surface, as well as effects resulting from Earth’s rotation, we take the direction of acceleration due to gravity to be downward (toward the center of Earth). In fact, its direction defines what we call vertical. Note that whether acceleration $a$ in the kinematic equations has the value $+g$ or $-g$ depends on how we define our coordinate system. If we define the upward direction as positive, then $a = -g = -9.8 m/s^2$, and if we define the downward direction as positive, then $a = g = 9.8 m/s^2$."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "blocks": [
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "when it is caught? Assume the ball is hit and caught at the same location."
    },
    {
      "type": "figure",
      "text": "FIGURE 3.28 A baseball hit straight up is caught by the catcher 5.0 s later. — Two illustrations of a baseball player on a baseball field. On the left, a batter hits the ball at $t = 0$ with a green upward velocity arrow, labeled $y = 0$. On the right, the catcher catches the ball at $t = 5.0\\text{ s}$ with a green downward velocity arrow, labeled $y = 0$.",
      "label": "FIGURE 3.28",
      "box": [94, 181, 353, 808]
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Strategy"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Choose a coordinate system with a positive $y$-axis that is straight up and with an origin that is at the spot where the ball is hit and caught."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Solution"
    },
    {
      "type": "list",
      "text": "a. Equation 3.16 gives\ny = y_0 + v_0 t - \\frac{1}{2}gt^2\n0 = 0 + v_0(5.0\\text{ s}) - \\frac{1}{2}(9.8\\text{ m/s}^2)(5.0\\text{ s})^2,\nwhich gives $v_0 = 24.5\\text{ m/s}$."
    },
    {
      "type": "list",
      "text": "b. At the maximum height, $v = 0$. With $v_0 = 24.5\\text{ m/s}$, Equation 3.17 gives\nv^2 = v_0^2 - 2g(y - y_0)\n0 = (24.5\\text{ m/s})^2 - 2(9.8\\text{ m/s}^2)(y - 0)\nor\ny = 30.6\\text{ m}."
    },
    {
      "type": "list",
      "text": "c. To find the time when $v = 0$, we use Equation 3.15:\nv = v_0 - gt\n0 = 24.5\\text{ m/s} - (9.8\\text{ m/s}^2)t.\nThis gives $t = 2.5\\text{ s}$. Since the ball rises for $2.5\\text{ s}$, the time to fall is $2.5\\text{ s}$."
    },
    {
      "type": "list",
      "text": "d. The acceleration is $9.8\\text{ m/s}^2$ everywhere, even when the velocity is zero at the top of the path. Although the velocity is zero at the top, it is changing at the rate of $9.8\\text{ m/s}^2$ downward."
    },
    {
      "type": "list",
      "text": "e. The velocity at $t = 5.0\\text{ s}$ can be determined with Equation 3.15:\nv = v_0 - gt\n= 24.5\\text{ m/s} - 9.8\\text{ m/s}^2(5.0\\text{ s})\n= -24.5\\text{ m/s}."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Significance"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "The ball returns with the speed it had when it left. This is a general property of free fall for any initial velocity. We used a single equation to go from throw to catch, and did not have to break the motion into two segments, upward"
    }
  ]
}

{
  "blocks": [
    {
      "type": "figure",
      "text": "FIGURE 3.27 The positions and velocities at 1-s intervals of a ball thrown downward from a tall building at 4.9 m/s. — A diagram showing a ball falling from a building with a table of time t (s), position x (m), and velocity v (m/s) at 1-s intervals from 0 to 4 s.",
      "label": "FIGURE 3.27",
      "box": [
        92,
        385,
        297,
        614
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Strategy"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Choose the origin at the top of the building with the positive direction upward and the negative direction downward. To find the time when the position is $-98 \\text{ m}$, we use Equation 3.16, with $y_0 = 0$, $v_0 = -4.9 \\text{ m/s}$, and $g = 9.8 \\text{ m/s}^2$."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Solution"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "a. Substitute the given values into the equation:"
    },
    {
      "type": "equation",
      "text": "y = y_0 + v_0t - \\frac{1}{2}gt^2 \\\\ -98.0 \\text{ m} = 0 - (4.9 \\text{ m/s})t - \\frac{1}{2}(9.8 \\text{ m/s}^2)t^2."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "This simplifies to"
    },
    {
      "type": "equation",
      "text": "t^2 + t - 20 = 0."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "This is a quadratic equation with roots $t = -5.0 \\text{ s}$ and $t = 4.0 \\text{ s}$. The positive root is the one we are interested in, since time $t = 0$ is the time when the ball is released at the top of the building. (The time $t = -5.0 \\text{ s}$ represents the fact that a ball thrown upward from the ground would have been in the air for 5.0 s when it passed by the top of the building moving downward at 4.9 m/s.)"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "b. Using Equation 3.15, we have"
    },
    {
      "type": "equation",
      "text": "v = v_0 - gt = -4.9 \\text{ m/s} - (9.8 \\text{ m/s}^2)(4.0 \\text{ s}) = -44.1 \\text{ m/s}."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Significance"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "For situations when two roots are obtained from a quadratic equation in the time variable, we must look at the physical significance of both roots to determine which is correct. Since $t = 0$ corresponds to the time when the ball was released, the negative root would correspond to a time before the ball was released, which is not physically meaningful. When the ball hits the ground, its velocity is not immediately zero, but as soon as the ball interacts with the ground, its acceleration is not $g$ and it accelerates with a different value over a short time to zero velocity. This problem shows how important it is to establish the correct coordinate system and to keep the signs of $g$ in the kinematic equations consistent."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "EXAMPLE 3.15"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Vertical Motion of a Baseball"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "A batter hits a baseball straight upward at home plate and the ball is caught 5.0 s after it is struck (Figure 3.28). (a) What is the initial velocity of the ball? (b) What is the maximum height the ball reaches? (c) How long does it take to reach the maximum height? (d) What is the acceleration at the top of its path? (e) What is the velocity of the ball"
    }
  ]
}

{
  "blocks": [
    {
      "type": "figure",
      "text": "FIGURE 3.20 U.S. Army Top Fuel pilot Tony “The Sarge” Schumacher begins a race with a controlled burnout. (credit: Lt. Col. William Thurmond. Photo Courtesy of U.S. Army.) — A dragster on a track with smoke billowing from its rear tires.",
      "label": "Figure 3.20",
      "box": [77, 305, 312, 692]
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Strategy"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "First, let’s draw a sketch (Figure 3.21). We are asked to find displacement, which is $x$ if we take $x_0$ to be zero. (Think about $x_0$ as the starting line of a race. It can be anywhere, but we call it zero and measure all other positions relative to it.) We can use the equation $x = x_0 + v_0t + \\frac{1}{2}at^2$ when we identify $v_0$, $a$, and $t$ from the statement of the problem."
    },
    {
      "type": "figure",
      "text": "FIGURE 3.21 Sketch of an accelerating dragster. — A schematic diagram showing a dragster moving from a starting position x0 to a final position x = ?, with an acceleration vector a = 26.0 m/s^2 pointing in the direction of motion, and a coordinate system showing x and y axes.",
      "label": "Figure 3.21",
      "box": [447, 193, 586, 806]
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Solution"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "First, we need to identify the knowns. Starting from rest means that $v_0 = 0$, $a$ is given as $26.0 \\text{ m/s}^2$ and $t$ is given as $5.56 \\text{ s}$."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Second, we substitute the known values into the equation to solve for the unknown:"
    },
    {
      "type": "equation",
      "text": "x = x_0 + v_0t + \\frac{1}{2}at^2"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Since the initial position and velocity are both zero, this equation simplifies to"
    },
    {
      "type": "equation",
      "text": "x = \\frac{1}{2}at^2"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Substituting the identified values of $a$ and $t$ gives"
    },
    {
      "type": "equation",
      "text": "x = \\frac{1}{2}(26.0 \\text{ m/s}^2)(5.56 \\text{ s})^2 = 402 \\text{ m}."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Significance"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "If we convert 402 m to miles, we find that the distance covered is very close to one-quarter of a mile, the standard distance for drag racing. So, our answer is reasonable. This is an impressive displacement to cover in only 5.56 s,"
    }
  ]
}

{
  "blocks": [
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Solution"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "a. From Equation 3.17, $v^2 = v_0^2 - 2g(y - y_0)$. With $v = 0$ and $y_0 = 0$, we can solve for y:"
    },
    {
      "type": "equation",
      "text": "y = \\frac{v_0^2}{2g} = \\frac{(2.0 \\times 10^2 \\text{ m/s})^2}{2(9.8 \\text{ m/s}^2)} = 2040.8 \\text{ m.}"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "This solution gives the maximum height of the booster in our coordinate system, which has its origin at the point of release, so the maximum height of the booster is roughly 7.0 km."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "b. An altitude of 6.0 km corresponds to $y = 1.0 \\times 10^3 \\text{ m}$ in the coordinate system we are using. The other initial conditions are $y_0 = 0$, and $v_0 = 200.0 \\text{ m/s}$. We have, from Equation 3.17,"
    },
    {
      "type": "equation",
      "text": "v^2 = (200.0 \\text{ m/s})^2 - 2(9.8 \\text{ m/s}^2)(1.0 \\times 10^3 \\text{ m}) \\implies v = \\pm 142.8 \\text{ m/s}."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Significance"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "We have both a positive and negative solution in (b). Since our coordinate system has the positive direction upward, the +142.8 m/s corresponds to a positive upward velocity at 6000 m during the upward leg of the trajectory of the booster. The value v = −142.8 m/s corresponds to the velocity at 6000 m on the downward leg. This example is also important in that an object is given an initial velocity at the origin of our coordinate system, but the origin is at an altitude above the surface of Earth, which must be taken into account when forming the solution."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "INTERACTIVE"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Engage the Phet simulation below to learn about graphing polynomials. The shape of the curve changes as the constants are adjusted. View the curves for the individual terms (for example, $y = bx$) to see how they add to generate the polynomial curve."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Access multimedia content (https://openstax.org/l/21equatgraph)"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "3.6 Finding Velocity and Displacement from Acceleration"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "LEARNING OBJECTIVES"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "By the end of this section, you will be able to:"
    },
    {
      "type": "list",
      "text": "• Derive the kinematic equations for constant acceleration using integral calculus.\n• Use the integral formulation of the kinematic equations in analyzing motion.\n• Find the functional form of velocity versus time given the acceleration function.\n• Find the functional form of position versus time given the velocity function."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "This section assumes you have enough background in calculus to be familiar with integration. In Instantaneous Velocity and Speed and Average and Instantaneous Acceleration we introduced the kinematic functions of velocity and acceleration using the derivative. By taking the derivative of the position function we found the velocity function, and likewise by taking the derivative of the velocity function we found the acceleration function. Using integral calculus, we can work backward and calculate the velocity function from the acceleration function, and the position function from the velocity function."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Kinematic Equations from Integral Calculus"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Let’s begin with a particle with an acceleration a(t) which is a known function of time. Since the time derivative of the velocity function is acceleration,"
    },
    {
      "type": "equation",
      "text": "\\frac{d}{dt}v(t) = a(t),"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "we can take the indefinite integral of both sides, finding"
    },
    {
      "type": "equation",
      "text": "\\int \\frac{d}{dt}v(t)dt = \\int a(t)dt + C_1,"
    }
  ]
}

{
  "blocks": [
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Strategy"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "(a) To get the velocity function we must integrate and use initial conditions to find the constant of integration. (b) We set the velocity function equal to zero and solve for t. (c) Similarly, we must integrate to find the position function and use initial conditions to find the constant of integration. (d) Since the initial position is taken to be zero, we only have to evaluate the position function at the time when the velocity is zero."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Solution"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "We take $t = 0$ to be the time when the boat starts to accelerate opposite to the motion."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "a. From the functional form of the acceleration we can solve Equation 3.18 to get $v(t)$:"
    },
    {
      "type": "equation",
      "text": "v(t) = \\int a(t)dt + C_1 = \\int \\left(-\\frac{1}{8}t \\text{ m/s}^3\\right) dt + C_1 = -\\frac{1}{8} \\text{ m/s}^3 t^2 + C_1."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "At $t = 0$ we have $v(0) = 5.0 \\text{ m/s} = 0 + C_1$, so $C_1 = 5.0 \\text{ m/s}$ or ."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "b. $v(t) = 0 = 5.0 \\text{ m/s} - \\frac{1}{8}t^2 \\text{ m/s}^3 \\Rightarrow t = 6.3 \\text{ s}$"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "c. Solve Equation 3.19:"
    },
    {
      "type": "equation",
      "text": "x(t) = \\int v(t)dt + C_2 = \\int \\left(5.0 - \\frac{1}{8}t^2\\right) dt + C_2 = 5.0t \\text{ m/s} - \\frac{1}{24}t^3 \\text{ m/s}^3 + C_2."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "At $t = 0$, we set $x(0) = 0 = x_0$, since we are only interested in the displacement from when the boat starts to accelerate opposite to the motion. We have"
    },
    {
      "type": "equation",
      "text": "x(0) = 0 = C_2."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Therefore, the equation for the position is"
    },
    {
      "type": "equation",
      "text": "x(t) = 5.0t \\text{ m/s} - \\frac{1}{24}t^3 \\text{ m/s}^3."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "d. Since the initial position is taken to be zero, we only have to evaluate the position function at the time when the velocity is zero. This occurs at $t = 6.3 \\text{ s}$. Therefore, the displacement is"
    },
    {
      "type": "equation",
      "text": "x(6.3) = 5.0t \\text{ m/s} (6.3 \\text{ s}) - \\frac{1}{24}t^3 \\text{ m/s}^3 (6.3 \\text{ s})^3 = 21.1 \\text{ m}."
    },
    {
      "type": "figure",
      "text": "FIGURE 3.30 (a) Velocity of the motorboat as a function of time. The motorboat decreases its velocity to zero in 6.3 s. At times greater than this, velocity becomes negative—meaning, the boat is reversing direction. (b) Position of the motorboat as a function of time. At t = 6.3 s, the velocity is zero and the boat has stopped. At times greater than this, the velocity becomes negative—meaning, if the boat continues to move with the same acceleration, it reverses direction and heads back toward where it originated. — Figure showing two graphs: (a) Velocity vs. Time with vertical axis v(t) (m/s) from 0 to 10 and horizontal axis Time (s) from 0 to 10. A curve starts at (0, 5.0) with equation v(t) = 5.0 - (1/8)t^2 and decreases to 0 at t = 6.3 s. (b) Position vs. Time with vertical axis x(t) (m) from 0 to 25 and horizontal axis Time (s) from 0 to 10. A curve starts at (0, 0) with equation x(t) = 5.0t - (1/24)t^3, peaks at t = 6.3 s, and then decreases.",
      "label": "FIGURE 3.30",
      "box": [
        531,
        155,
        833,
        839
      ]
    }
  ]
}

{
  "blocks": [
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "where $C_1$ is a constant of integration. Since $\\int \\frac{d}{dt} v(t) dt = v(t)$, the velocity is given by"
    },
    {
      "type": "equation",
      "text": "v(t) = \\int a(t) dt + C_1.",
      "label": "3.18"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Similarly, the time derivative of the position function is the velocity function,"
    },
    {
      "type": "equation",
      "text": "\\frac{d}{dt} x(t) = v(t)."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Thus, we can use the same mathematical manipulations we just used and find"
    },
    {
      "type": "equation",
      "text": "x(t) = \\int v(t) dt + C_2,",
      "label": "3.19"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "where $C_2$ is a second constant of integration."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "We can derive the kinematic equations for a constant acceleration using these integrals. With $a(t) = a$ a constant, and doing the integration in Equation 3.18, we find"
    },
    {
      "type": "equation",
      "text": "v(t) = \\int a dt + C_1 = at + C_1."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "If the initial velocity is $v(0) = v_0$, then"
    },
    {
      "type": "equation",
      "text": "v_0 = 0 + C_1."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Then, $C_1 = v_0$ and"
    },
    {
      "type": "equation",
      "text": "v(t) = v_0 + at,"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "which is Equation 3.12. Substituting this expression into Equation 3.19 gives"
    },
    {
      "type": "equation",
      "text": "x(t) = \\int (v_0 + at) dt + C_2."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Doing the integration, we find"
    },
    {
      "type": "equation",
      "text": "x(t) = v_0 t + \\frac{1}{2} at^2 + C_2."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "If $x(0) = x_0$, we have"
    },
    {
      "type": "equation",
      "text": "x_0 = 0 + 0 + C_2;"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "so, $C_2 = x_0$. Substituting back into the equation for $x(t)$, we finally have"
    },
    {
      "type": "equation",
      "text": "x(t) = x_0 + v_0 t + \\frac{1}{2} at^2,"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "which is Equation 3.13."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "EXAMPLE 3.17"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Motion of a Motorboat"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "A motorboat is traveling at a constant velocity of $5.0\\text{ m/s}$ when it starts to accelerate opposite to the motion to arrive at the dock. Its acceleration is $a(t) = -\\frac{1}{4}t\\text{ m/s}^3$. (a) What is the velocity function of the motorboat? (b) At what time does the velocity reach zero? (c) What is the position function of the motorboat? (d) What is the displacement of the motorboat from the time it begins to accelerate opposite to the motion to when the velocity is zero? (e) Graph the velocity and position functions."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "blocks": [
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Chapter Review"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Key Terms"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "acceleration due to gravity acceleration of an object as a result of gravity"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "average acceleration the rate of change in velocity; the change in velocity over time"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "average speed the total distance traveled divided by elapsed time"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "average velocity the displacement divided by the time over which displacement occurs under constant acceleration"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "displacement the change in position of an object"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "distance traveled the total length of the path traveled between two positions"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "elapsed time the difference between the ending time and the beginning time"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "free fall the state of movement that results from gravitational force only"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "instantaneous acceleration acceleration at a specific point in time"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "instantaneous speed the absolute value of the instantaneous velocity"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "instantaneous velocity the velocity at a specific instant or time point"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "kinematics the description of motion through properties such as position, time, velocity, and acceleration"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "position the location of an object at a particular time"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "total displacement the sum of individual displacements over a given time period"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "two-body pursuit problem a kinematics problem in which the unknowns are calculated by solving the kinematic equations simultaneously for two moving objects"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Key Equations"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Displacement"
    },
    {
      "type": "equation",
      "text": "\\Delta x = x_f - x_i"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Total displacement"
    },
    {
      "type": "equation",
      "text": "\\Delta x_{\\text{Total}} = \\sum \\Delta x_i"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Average velocity"
    },
    {
      "type": "equation",
      "text": "\\bar{v} = \\frac{\\Delta x}{\\Delta t} = \\frac{x_2 - x_1}{t_2 - t_1}"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Instantaneous velocity"
    },
    {
      "type": "equation",
      "text": "v(t) = \\frac{dx(t)}{dt}"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Average speed"
    },
    {
      "type": "equation",
      "text": "\\text{Average speed} = \\bar{s} = \\frac{\\text{Total distance}}{\\text{Elapsed time}}"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Instantaneous speed"
    },
    {
      "type": "equation",
      "text": "\\text{Instantaneous speed} = |v(t)|"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Average acceleration"
    },
    {
      "type": "equation",
      "text": "\\bar{a} = \\frac{\\Delta v}{\\Delta t} = \\frac{v_f - v_0}{t_f - t_0}"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Instantaneous acceleration"
    },
    {
      "type": "equation",
      "text": "a(t) = \\frac{dv(t)}{dt}"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Position from average velocity"
    },
    {
      "type": "equation",
      "text": "x = x_0 + \\bar{v}t"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Average velocity (for constant acceleration)"
    },
    {
      "type": "equation",
      "text": "\\bar{v} = \\frac{v_0 + v}{2}"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Velocity from acceleration"
    },
    {
      "type": "equation",
      "text": "v = v_0 + at \\text{ (constant } a\\text{)}"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Position from velocity and acceleration"
    },
    {
      "type": "equation",
      "text": "x = x_0 + v_0 t + \\frac{1}{2}at^2 \\text{ (constant } a\\text{)}"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Velocity from distance"
    },
    {
      "type": "equation",
      "text": "v^2 = v_0^2 + 2a(x - x_0) \\text{ (constant } a\\text{)}"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Velocity of free fall"
    },
    {
      "type": "equation",
      "text": "v = v_0 - gt \\text{ (positive upward)}"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Height of free fall"
    },
    {
      "type": "equation",
      "text": "y = y_0 + v_0 t - \\frac{1}{2}gt^2"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Velocity of free fall from height"
    },
    {
      "type": "equation",
      "text": "v^2 = v_0^2 - 2g(y - y_0)"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Velocity from acceleration"
    },
    {
      "type": "equation",
      "text": "v(t) = \\int a(t)dt + C_1"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Position from velocity"
    },
    {
      "type": "equation",
      "text": "x(t) = \\int v(t)dt + C_2"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Summary"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "3.1 Position, Displacement, and Average Velocity"
    },
    {
      "type": "list",
      "text": "Kinematics is the description of motion without considering its causes. In this chapter, it is limited to motion along a straight line, called one-dimensional motion."
    },
    {
      "type": "list",
      "text": "Displacement is the change in position of an object. The SI unit for displacement is the meter."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "blocks": [
    {
      "type": "list",
      "text": "For a fixed acceleration, a car that is going twice as fast doesn’t simply stop in twice the distance. It takes much farther to stop. (This is why we have reduced speed zones near schools.)"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Putting Equations Together"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "In the following examples, we continue to explore one-dimensional motion, but in situations requiring slightly more algebraic manipulation. The examples also give insight into problem-solving techniques. The note that follows is provided for easy reference to the equations needed. Be aware that these equations are not independent. In many situations we have two unknowns and need two equations from the set to solve for the unknowns. We need as many equations as there are unknowns to solve a given situation."
    },
    {
      "type": "table",
      "text": "SUMMARY OF KINEMATIC EQUATIONS (CONSTANT A)\n\n| |\n| :--- |\n| $x = x_0 + \\bar{v}t$ |\n| $\\bar{v} = \\frac{v_0 + v}{2}$ |\n| $v = v_0 + at$ |\n| $x = x_0 + v_0t + \\frac{1}{2}at^2$ |\n| $v^2 = v_0^2 + 2a(x - x_0)$ |"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Before we get into the examples, let’s look at some of the equations more closely to see the behavior of acceleration at extreme values. Rearranging Equation 3.12, we have"
    },
    {
      "type": "equation",
      "text": "a = \\frac{v - v_0}{t}"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "From this we see that, for a finite time, if the difference between the initial and final velocities is small, the acceleration is small, approaching zero in the limit that the initial and final velocities are equal. On the contrary, in the limit $t \\to 0$ for a finite difference between the initial and final velocities, acceleration becomes infinite."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Similarly, rearranging Equation 3.14, we can express acceleration in terms of velocities and displacement:"
    },
    {
      "type": "equation",
      "text": "a = \\frac{v^2 - v_0^2}{2(x - x_0)}"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Thus, for a finite difference between the initial and final velocities acceleration becomes infinite in the limit the displacement approaches zero. Acceleration approaches zero in the limit the difference in initial and final velocities approaches zero for a finite displacement."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "EXAMPLE 3.10"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
