      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "The steps given here are sufficient to guide you in this important problem-solving strategy. The final section of this chapter explains in more detail how to draw free-body diagrams when working with the ideas presented in this chapter."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Development of the Force Concept"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "A quantitative definition of force can be based on some standard force, just as distance is measured in units relative to a standard length. One possibility is to stretch a spring a certain fixed distance (Figure 5.5) and use the force it exerts to pull itself back to its relaxed shape—called a restoring force—as a standard. The magnitude of all other forces can be considered as multiples of this standard unit of force. Many other possibilities exist for standard forces. Some alternative definitions of force will be given later in this chapter."
    },
    {
      "type": "figure",
      "text": "FIGURE 5.5 The force exerted by a stretched spring can be used as a standard unit of force. (a) This spring has a length x when undistorted. (b) When stretched a distance $\\Delta x$, the spring exerts a restoring force $\\vec{F}_{restore}$, which is reproducible. (c) A spring scale is one device that uses a spring to measure force. The force is exerted on whatever is attached to the hook. Here, this force has a magnitude of six units of the force standard being employed. — Three parts showing a spring. (a) A spring of length x. (b) A spring stretched by $\\Delta x$ with a hand pulling it, showing a vector $\\vec{F}_{restore}$ pointing opposite to the stretch. (c) A spring scale showing a pointer at the number 6, with a vector $\\vec{F}_{restore}$ pointing left.",
      "label": "Figure 5.5",
      "box": [
        358,
        195,
        648,
        804
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Let’s analyze force more deeply. Suppose a physics student sits at a table, working diligently on his homework (Figure 5.6). What external forces act on him? Can we determine the origin of these forces?"
    }
  ]
}

{
  "blocks": [
    {
      "type": "figure",
      "text": "Figure 5.3 — A top-down view of a car being pulled by two forces. The car is at the bottom, with two force vectors originating from its center. One vector points up and to the left with a magnitude of 380.0 N at an angle of 10 degrees from the vertical. The other vector points up and to the right with a magnitude of 450.0 N at an angle of 30 degrees from the vertical.",
      "label": "Figure 5.3",
      "box": [
        68,
        116,
        278,
        333
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Newton’s Second Law and Momentum"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Newton actually stated his second law in terms of momentum: “The instantaneous rate at which a body’s momentum changes is equal to the net force acting on the body.” (“Instantaneous rate” implies that the derivative is involved.) This can be given by the vector equation"
    },
    {
      "type": "equation",
      "text": "\\vec{F}_{net} = \\frac{d\\vec{p}}{dt}",
      "label": "5.6"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "This means that Newton’s second law addresses the central question of motion: What causes a change in motion of an object? Momentum was described by Newton as “quantity of motion,” a way of combining both the velocity of an object and its mass. We devote Linear Momentum and Collisions to the study of momentum."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "For now, it is sufficient to define momentum $\\vec{p}$ as the product of the mass of the object $m$ and its velocity $\\vec{v}$:"
    },
    {
      "type": "equation",
      "text": "\\vec{p} = m\\vec{v}",
      "label": "5.7"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Since velocity is a vector, so is momentum."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "It is easy to visualize momentum. A train moving at 10 m/s has more momentum than one that moves at 2 m/s. In everyday life, we speak of one sports team as “having momentum” when they score points against the opposing team."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "If we substitute Equation 5.7 into Equation 5.6, we obtain"
    },
    {
      "type": "equation",
      "text": "\\vec{F}_{net} = \\frac{d\\vec{p}}{dt} = \\frac{d(m\\vec{v})}{dt}"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "When $m$ is constant, we have"
    },
    {
      "type": "equation",
      "text": "\\vec{F}_{net} = m\\frac{d(\\vec{v})}{dt} = m\\vec{a}"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Thus, we see that the momentum form of Newton’s second law reduces to the form given earlier in this section."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "INTERACTIVE"
