{
  "blocks": [
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "There are two important features of Newton’s third law. First, the forces exerted (the action and reaction) are always equal in magnitude but opposite in direction. Second, these forces are acting on different bodies or systems: A’s force acts on B and B’s force acts on A. In other words, the two forces are distinct forces that do not act on the same body. Thus, they do not cancel each other."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "For the situation shown in Figure 5.6, the third law indicates that because the chair is pushing upward on the boy with force $\\vec{C}$, he is pushing downward on the chair with force $-\\vec{C}$. Similarly, he is pushing downward with forces $-\\vec{F}$ and $-\\vec{T}$ on the floor and table, respectively. Finally, since Earth pulls downward on the boy with force $\\vec{W}$, he pulls upward on Earth with force $-\\vec{W}$. If that student were to angrily pound the table in frustration, he would quickly learn the painful lesson (avoidable by studying Newton’s laws) that the table hits back just as hard."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "A person who is walking or running applies Newton’s third law instinctively. For example, the runner in Figure 5.18 pushes backward on the ground so that it pushes him forward."
    },
    {
      "type": "figure",
      "text": "FIGURE 5.18 The runner experiences Newton’s third law. (a) A force is exerted by the runner on the ground. (b) The reaction force of the ground on the runner pushes him forward. (credit \"runner\": modification of work by \"Greenwich Photography\"/Flickr) — Two diagrams of a runner in a red shirt and dark shorts. (a) Shows the runner pushing backward and downward on the ground with a force vector labeled $\\vec{F}$, with text below reading \"Runner pushes back and down on ground\". (b) Shows the runner with a ground reaction force vector pointing up and forward labeled $-\\vec{F}$, with text below reading \"Ground pushes forward and up on runner\".",
      "label": "FIGURE 5.18",
      "box": [
