      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "The scale actually does not measure the weight of the package; it measures the force $-\\vec{S}$ on its surface. If the system is accelerating, $\\vec{S}$ and $-\\vec{w}$ would not be equal, as explained in Applications of Newton’s Laws."
    },
    {
      "type": "example",
      "text": "EXAMPLE 5.10 Getting Up to Speed: Choosing the Correct System A physics professor pushes a cart of demonstration equipment to a lecture hall (Figure 5.20). Her mass is 65.0 kg, the cart’s mass is 12.0 kg, and the equipment’s mass is 7.0 kg. Calculate the acceleration produced when the professor exerts a backward force of 150 N on the floor. All forces opposing the motion, such as friction on the cart’s wheels and air resistance, total 24.0 N."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "blocks": [
    {
      "type": "figure",
      "text": "FIGURE 5.22 Since the acceleration is parallel to the slope and acting down the slope, it is most convenient to project all forces onto a coordinate system where one axis is parallel to the slope and the other is perpendicular to it (axes shown to the left of the skier). — A skier on an inclined slope at 25 degrees to the horizontal. A coordinate system with $x$ and $y$ axes is shown to the left. The forces acting on the skier are: weight $\\vec{w}$ acting straight down, its components $\\vec{w}_x$ (parallel to the slope, downslope) and $\\vec{w}_y$ (perpendicular to the slope), normal force $\\vec{N}$ perpendicular to the slope pointing up-right, and friction force $\\vec{f}$ parallel to the slope pointing up-slope. A separate free-body diagram on the right shows all these force vectors meeting at a common origin.",
      "label": "FIGURE 5.22",
      "box": [
        57,
        155,
        342,
        843
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "FIGURE 5.22 Since the acceleration is parallel to the slope and acting down the slope, it is most convenient to project all forces onto a coordinate system where one axis is parallel to the slope and the other is perpendicular to it (axes shown to the left of the skier). $\\vec{N}$ is perpendicular to the slope and $\\vec{f}$ is parallel to the slope, but $\\vec{w}$ has components along both axes, namely, $w_y$ and $w_x$. Here, $\\vec{w}$ has a squiggly line to show that it has been replaced by these components. The force $\\vec{N}$ is equal in magnitude to $w_y$, so there is no acceleration perpendicular to the slope, but $f$ is less than $w_x$, so there is a downslope acceleration (along the axis parallel to the slope)."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Strategy"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "This is a two-dimensional problem, since not all forces on the skier (the system of interest) are parallel. The approach we have used in two-dimensional kinematics also works well here. Choose a convenient coordinate system and project the vectors onto its axes, creating two one-dimensional problems to solve. The most convenient coordinate system for motion on an incline is one that has one coordinate parallel to the slope and one perpendicular to the slope. (Motions along mutually perpendicular axes are independent.) We use $x$ and $y$ for the parallel and perpendicular directions, respectively. This choice of axes simplifies this type of problem, because there is no motion perpendicular to the slope and the acceleration is downslope. Regarding the forces, friction is drawn in opposition to motion (friction always opposes forward motion) and is always parallel to the slope, $w_x$ is drawn parallel to the slope and downslope (it causes the motion of the skier down the slope), and $w_y$ is drawn as the component of weight perpendicular to the slope. Then, we can consider the separate problems of forces parallel to the slope and forces perpendicular to the slope."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Solution"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "The magnitude of the component of weight parallel to the slope is"
    },
    {
      "type": "equation",
      "text": "w_x = w \\sin 25^{\\circ} = mg \\sin 25^{\\circ},"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "and the magnitude of the component of the weight perpendicular to the slope is"
    },
    {
      "type": "equation",
      "text": "w_y = w \\cos 25^{\\circ} = mg \\cos 25^{\\circ}."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "a. Neglect friction. Since the acceleration is parallel to the slope, we need only consider forces parallel to the slope. (Forces perpendicular to the slope add to zero, since there is no acceleration in that direction.) The forces parallel to the slope are the component of the skier’s weight parallel to slope $w_x$ and friction $f$. Using Newton’s second law, with subscripts to denote quantities parallel to the slope,"
    },
    {
      "type": "equation",
      "text": "a_x = \\frac{F_{\\text{net } x}}{m}"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "where $F_{\\text{net } x} = w_x = -mg \\sin 25^{\\circ}$, assuming no friction for this part. Therefore,"
    }
  ]
}

{
  "blocks": [
    {
      "type": "equation",
      "text": "a_{x} = \\frac{F_{\\text{net } x}}{m} = \\frac{mg \\sin 25^{\\circ}}{m} = g \\sin 25^{\\circ} \\\\\n(9.80 \\text{ m/s}^{2})(0.4226) = 4.14 \\text{ m/s}^{2}"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "is the acceleration."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "b. Include friction. We have a given value for friction, and we know its direction is parallel to the slope and it opposes motion between surfaces in contact. So the net external force is"
    },
    {
      "type": "equation",
      "text": "F_{\\text{net } x} = w_{x} - f."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Substituting this into Newton's second law, $a_{x} = F_{\\text{net } x} / m$, gives"
    },
    {
      "type": "equation",
      "text": "a_{x} = \\frac{F_{\\text{net } x}}{m} = \\frac{w_{x} - f}{m} = \\frac{mg \\sin 25^{\\circ} - f}{m}."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "We substitute known values to obtain"
    },
    {
      "type": "equation",
      "text": "a_{x} = \\frac{(60.0 \\text{ kg})(9.80 \\text{ m/s}^{2})(0.4226) - 45.0 \\text{ N}}{60.0 \\text{ kg}}."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "This gives us"
    },
    {
      "type": "equation",
      "text": "a_{x} = 3.39 \\text{ m/s}^{2},"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "which is the acceleration parallel to the incline when there is $45.0 \\text{ N}$ of opposing friction."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Significance"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Since friction always opposes motion between surfaces, the acceleration is smaller when there is friction than when there is none. It is a general result that if friction on an incline is negligible, then the acceleration down the incline is $a = g \\sin \\theta$, regardless of mass. As discussed previously, all objects fall with the same acceleration in the absence of air resistance. Similarly, all objects, regardless of mass, slide down a frictionless incline with the same acceleration (if the angle is the same)."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "When an object rests on an incline that makes an angle $\\theta$ with the horizontal, the force of gravity acting on the object is divided into two components: a force acting perpendicular to the plane, $w_{y}$, and a force acting parallel to the plane, $w_{x}$ (Figure 5.23). The normal force $\\vec{N}$ is typically equal in magnitude and opposite in direction to the perpendicular component of the weight $w_{y}$. The force acting parallel to the plane, $w_{x}$, causes the object to accelerate down the incline."
    },
    {
      "type": "figure",
      "text": "FIGURE 5.23 An object rests on an incline that makes an angle \\theta with the horizontal. — A diagram showing an inclined plane at an angle $\\theta$ to the horizontal. A rectangular object rests on the incline. A coordinate system $x$-$y$ is tilted with the $x$-axis parallel to the incline and the $y$-axis perpendicular to it. The weight vector $\\vec{w}$ points straight down, and is resolved into components $\\vec{w}_x$ (parallel to the incline) and $\\vec{w}_y$ (perpendicular to the incline). Equations on the right show $w_{x} = w \\sin(\\theta) = mg \\sin(\\theta)$ and $w_{y} = w \\cos(\\theta) = mg \\cos(\\theta)$.",
      "label": "Figure 5.23",
      "box": [680, 252, 846, 755]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Be careful when resolving the weight of the object into components. If the incline is at an angle $\\theta$ to the horizontal, then the magnitudes of the weight components are"
    },
    {
      "type": "equation",
      "text": "w_{x} = w \\sin \\theta = mg \\sin \\theta"
    }
  ]
}

{
  "blocks": [
    {
      "type": "figure",
      "text": "FIGURE 5.24 — A diagram showing a hand holding a rope attached to a mass m, alongside a free-body diagram of the mass. The rope shows downward tension forces \\vec{T} from the hand and upward tension \\vec{T} on the hand, and downward weight \\vec{w} and upward tension \\vec{T} on the mass m. The free-body diagram shows an upward vector \\vec{T} and a downward vector \\.",
      "label": "FIGURE 5.24",
      "box": [71, 373, 356, 626]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "FIGURE 5.24 When a perfectly flexible connector (one requiring no force to bend it) such as this rope transmits a force $\\vec{T}$, that force must be parallel to the length of the rope, as shown. By Newton’s third law, the rope pulls with equal force but in opposite directions on the hand and the supported mass (neglecting the weight of the rope). The rope is the medium that carries the equal and opposite forces between the two objects. The tension anywhere in the rope between the hand and the mass is equal. Once you have determined the tension in one location, you have determined the tension at all locations along the rope."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Flexible connectors are often used to transmit forces around corners, such as in a hospital traction system, a tendon, or a bicycle brake cable. If there is no friction, the tension transmission is undiminished; only its direction changes, and it is always parallel to the flexible connector, as shown in Figure 5.25."
    },
    {
      "type": "figure",
      "text": "FIGURE 5.25 (a) Tendons in the finger carry force T from the muscles to other parts of the finger, usually changing the force’s direction but not its magnitude (the tendons are relatively friction free). (b) The brake cable on a bicycle carries the tension T from the brake lever on the handlebars to the brake mechanism. Again, the direction but not the magnitude of T is changed. — Two illustrations: (a) An anatomical diagram of a finger showing extensor muscles, extensor tendons, and flexor tendons with tension force vectors \\vec{T}. (b) A bicycle with red arrows indicating tension \\vec{T} along the brake cable and at the handlebars.",
      "label": "FIGURE 5.25",
      "box": [488, 117, 690, 853]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "FIGURE 5.25 (a) Tendons in the finger carry force T from the muscles to other parts of the finger, usually changing the force’s direction but not its magnitude (the tendons are relatively friction free). (b) The brake cable on a bicycle carries the tension T from the brake lever on the handlebars to the brake mechanism. Again, the direction but not the magnitude of T is changed."
    },
    {
      "type": "example",
      "text": "EXAMPLE 5.13 \n\nWhat Is the Tension in a Tightrope? \n\nCalculate the tension in the wire supporting the 70.0-kg tightrope walker shown in Figure 5.26."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "blocks": [
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "and"
    },
    {
      "type": "equation",
      "text": "w_y = w \\cos \\theta = mg \\cos \\theta."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "We use the second equation to write the normal force experienced by an object resting on an inclined plane:"
    },
    {
      "type": "equation",
      "text": "N = mg \\cos \\theta,"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "object at rest or sliding on an inclined plane. Instead of memorizing these equations, it is helpful to be able to determine them from reason. To do this, we draw the right angle formed by the three weight vectors. The angle $\\theta$ of the incline is the same as the angle formed between $w$ and $w_y$. Knowing this property, we can use trigonometry to determine the magnitude of the weight components:"
    },
    {
      "type": "equation",
      "text": "\\cos \\theta = \\frac{w_y}{w}, w_y = w \\cos \\theta = mg \\cos \\theta \\\\ \\sin \\theta = \\frac{w_x}{w}, w_x = w \\sin \\theta = mg \\sin \\theta."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "CHECK YOUR UNDERSTANDING 5.8"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "A force of 1150 N acts parallel to a ramp to push a 250-kg gun safe into a moving van. The ramp is frictionless and inclined at $17^\\circ$. (a) What is the acceleration of the safe up the ramp? (b) If we consider friction in this problem, with a friction force of 120 N, what is the acceleration of the safe?"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Tension"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "A tension is a force along the length of a medium; in particular, it is a pulling force that acts along a stretched flexible connector, such as a rope or cable. The word “tension” comes from a Latin word meaning “to stretch.” Not coincidentally, the flexible cords that carry muscle forces to other parts of the body are called tendons."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Any flexible connector, such as a string, rope, chain, wire, or cable, can only exert a pull parallel to its length; thus, a force carried by a flexible connector is a tension with a direction parallel to the connector. Tension is a pull in a connector. Consider the phrase: “You can’t push a rope.” Instead, tension force pulls outward along the two ends of a rope."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Consider a person holding a mass on a rope, as shown in Figure 5.24. If the 5.00-kg mass in the figure is stationary, then its acceleration is zero and the net force is zero. The only external forces acting on the mass are its weight and the tension supplied by the rope. Thus,"
    },
    {
      "type": "equation",
      "text": "F_{\\text{net}} = T - w = 0,"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "where $T$ and $w$ are the magnitudes of the tension and weight, respectively, and their signs indicate direction, with up being positive. As we proved using Newton’s second law, the tension equals the weight of the supported mass:"
    },
    {
      "type": "equation",
      "text": "T = w = mg,"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "object hanging at rest. Thus, for a 5.00-kg mass (neglecting the mass of the rope), we see that"
    },
    {
      "type": "equation",
      "text": "T = mg = (5.00 \\text{ kg})(9.80 \\text{ m/s}^2) = 49.0 \\text{ N}."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "If we cut the rope and insert a spring, the spring would extend a length corresponding to a force of 49.0 N, providing a direct observation and measure of the tension force in the rope."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "blocks": [
    {
      "type": "equation",
      "text": "F_{\\text{net } x} = 0 = T_{\\text{R}x} - T_{\\text{L}x} \\\\\nT_{\\text{L}x} = T_{\\text{R}x}"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Now observe Figure 5.27. You can use trigonometry to determine the magnitude of $T_L$ and $T_R$:"
    },
    {
      "type": "equation",
      "text": "\\cos 5.0^\\circ = \\frac{T_{\\text{L}x}}{T_L}, \\quad T_{\\text{L}x} = T_L \\cos 5.0^\\circ \\\\\n\\cos 5.0^\\circ = \\frac{T_{\\text{R}x}}{T_R}, \\quad T_{\\text{R}x} = T_R \\cos 5.0^\\circ"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Equating $T_{\\text{L}x}$ and $T_{\\text{R}x}$:"
    },
    {
      "type": "equation",
      "text": "T_L \\cos 5.0^\\circ = T_R \\cos 5.0^\\circ"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Thus,"
    },
    {
      "type": "equation",
      "text": "T_L = T_R = T,"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "as predicted. Now, considering the vertical components (denoted by a subscript y), we can solve for $T$. Again, since the person is stationary, Newton's second law implies that $F_{\\text{net } y} = 0$. Thus, as illustrated in the free-body diagram,"
    },
    {
      "type": "equation",
      "text": "F_{\\text{net } y} = T_{\\text{L}y} + T_{\\text{R}y} - w = 0."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "We can use trigonometry to determine the relationships among $T_{\\text{L}y}$, $T_{\\text{R}y}$, and $T$. As we determined from the analysis in the horizontal direction, $T_L = T_R = T$:"
    },
    {
      "type": "equation",
      "text": "\\sin 5.0^\\circ = \\frac{T_{\\text{L}y}}{T_L}, \\quad T_{\\text{L}y} = T_L \\sin 5.0^\\circ = T \\sin 5.0^\\circ \\\\\n\\sin 5.0^\\circ = \\frac{T_{\\text{R}y}}{T_R}, \\quad T_{\\text{R}y} = T_R \\sin 5.0^\\circ = T \\sin 5.0^\\circ."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Now we can substitute the vales for $T_{\\text{L}y}$ and $T_{\\text{R}y}$, into the net force equation in the vertical direction:"
    },
    {
      "type": "equation",
      "text": "F_{\\text{net } y} = T_{\\text{L}y} + T_{\\text{R}y} - w = 0 \\\\\nF_{\\text{net } y} = T \\sin 5.0^\\circ + T \\sin 5.0^\\circ - w = 0 \\\\\n2T \\sin 5.0^\\circ - w = 0 \\\\\n2T \\sin 5.0^\\circ = w"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "and"
    },
    {
      "type": "equation",
      "text": "T = \\frac{w}{2 \\sin 5.0^\\circ} = \\frac{mg}{2 \\sin 5.0^\\circ},"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "so"
    },
    {
      "type": "equation",
      "text": "T = \\frac{(70.0 \\text{ kg})(9.80 \\text{ m/s}^2)}{2(0.0872)},"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "and the tension is"
    },
    {
      "type": "equation",
      "text": "T = 3930 \\text{ N}."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Significance"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "The vertical tension in the wire acts as a force that supports the weight of the tightrope walker. The tension is almost six times the 686-N weight of the tightrope walker. Since the wire is nearly horizontal, the vertical component of its tension is only a fraction of the tension in the wire. The large horizontal components are in opposite directions and cancel, so most of the tension in the wire is not used to support the weight of the tightrope walker."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "If we wish to create a large tension, all we have to do is exert a force perpendicular to a taut flexible connector, as"
    }
  ]
}

{
  "blocks": [
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "illustrated in Figure 5.26. As we saw in Example 5.13, the weight of the tightrope walker acts as a force perpendicular to the rope. We saw that the tension in the rope is related to the weight of the tightrope walker in the following way:"
    },
    {
      "type": "equation",
      "text": "T = \\frac{w}{2 \\sin \\theta}"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "We can extend this expression to describe the tension T created when a perpendicular force ($F_\\perp$) is exerted at the middle of a flexible connector:"
    },
    {
      "type": "equation",
      "text": "T = \\frac{F_\\perp}{2 \\sin \\theta}"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "The angle between the horizontal and the bent connector is represented by $\\theta$. In this case, T becomes large as $\\theta$ approaches zero. Even the relatively small weight of any flexible connector will cause it to sag, since an infinite tension would result if it were horizontal (i.e., $\\theta = 0$ and $\\sin \\theta = 0$). For example, Figure 5.28 shows a situation where we wish to pull a car out of the mud when no tow truck is available. Each time the car moves forward, the chain is tightened to keep it as straight as possible. The tension in the chain is given by $T = \\frac{F_\\perp}{2 \\sin \\theta}$, and since $\\theta$ is small, T is large. This situation is analogous to the tightrope walker, except that the tensions shown here are those transmitted to the car and the tree rather than those acting at the point where $F_\\perp$ is applied."
    },
    {
      "type": "figure",
      "text": "FIGURE 5.28 We can create a large tension in the chain—and potentially a big mess—by pushing on it perpendicular to its length, as shown. — A top-down illustration showing a blue car stuck in mud on the left, connected via a taut chain with a red tension vector pointing left labelled T to a tree trunk on the right with a red tension vector pointing right labelled T. At the midpoint of the chain, a perpendicular downward force vector $F_\\perp$ is applied, bending the chain into an angle $\\theta$ with the horizontal on both sides.",
      "label": "FIGURE 5.28",
      "box": [355, 117, 477, 882]
    },
