      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Summary"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "5.1 Forces"
    },
    {
      "type": "list",
      "text": "Dynamics is the study of how forces affect the motion of objects, whereas kinematics simply describes the way objects move."
    },
    {
      "type": "list",
      "text": "Force is a push or pull that can be defined in terms of various standards, and it is a vector that has both magnitude and direction."
    },
    {
      "type": "list",
      "text": "External forces are any outside forces that act on a body. A free-body diagram is a drawing of all external forces acting on a body."
    },
    {
      "type": "list",
      "text": "The SI unit of force is the newton (N)."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "5.2 Newton's First Law"
    },
    {
      "type": "list",
      "text": "According to Newton’s first law, there must be a cause for any change in velocity (a change in either magnitude or direction) to occur. This law is also known as the law of inertia."
    },
    {
      "type": "list",
      "text": "Friction is an external force that causes an object to slow down."
    },
    {
      "type": "list",
      "text": "Inertia is the tendency of an object to remain at rest or remain in motion. Inertia is related to an object’s mass."
    },
    {
      "type": "list",
      "text": "If an object’s velocity relative to a given frame is constant, then the frame is inertial. This means that for an inertial reference frame, Newton’s first law is valid."
    },
    {
      "type": "list",
      "text": "Equilibrium is achieved when the forces on a system are balanced."
    },
    {
      "type": "list",
      "text": "A net force of zero means that an object is either at rest or moving with constant velocity; that is, it is not accelerating."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "5.3 Newton's Second Law"
    },
    {
      "type": "list",
      "text": "An external force acts on a system from outside the system, as opposed to internal forces, which act between components within the system."
    },
    {
      "type": "list",
      "text": "Newton’s second law of motion says that the net external force on an object with a certain mass is directly proportional to and in the same direction as the acceleration of the object."
    },
    {
      "type": "list",
      "text": "Newton’s second law can also describe net force as the instantaneous rate of change of momentum. Thus, a net external force causes nonzero acceleration."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "5.4 Mass and Weight"
    },
    {
      "type": "list",
      "text": "Mass is the quantity of matter in a substance."
    },
    {
      "type": "list",
      "text": "The weight of an object is the net force on a falling object, or its gravitational force. The object experiences acceleration due to gravity."
    },
    {
      "type": "list",
      "text": "Some upward resistance force from the air acts on all falling objects on Earth, so they can never truly be in free fall."
    },
    {
      "type": "list",
      "text": "Careful distinctions must be made between free fall and weightlessness using the definition of weight as force due to gravity acting on an object of a certain mass."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "5.5 Newton's Third Law"
    },
    {
      "type": "list",
      "text": "Newton’s third law of motion represents a basic symmetry in nature, with an experienced force equal in magnitude and opposite in direction to an exerted force."
    },
    {
      "type": "list",
      "text": "Two equal and opposite forces do not cancel because they act on different systems."
    },
    {
      "type": "list",
      "text": "Action-reaction pairs include a swimmer pushing off a wall, helicopters creating lift by pushing air down, and an octopus propelling itself forward by ejecting water from its body. Rockets, airplanes, and cars are pushed forward by a thrust reaction force."
    },
    {
      "type": "list",
      "text": "Choosing a system is an important analytical step in understanding the physics of a problem and solving it."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "5.6 Common Forces"
    },
    {
      "type": "list",
      "text": "When an object rests on a surface, the surface applies a force to the object that supports the weight of the object. This supporting force acts perpendicular to and away from the surface. It is called a normal force."
    },
    {
      "type": "list",
