    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Friction and the Inclined Plane"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "One situation where friction plays an obvious role is that of an object on a slope. It might be a crate being pushed up a ramp to a loading dock or a skateboarder coasting down a mountain, but the basic physics is the same. We usually generalize the sloping surface and call it an inclined plane but then pretend that the surface is flat. Let's look at an example of analyzing motion on an inclined plane with friction."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "EXAMPLE 6.11"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Downhill Skier"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "A skier with a mass of $62\\text{ kg}$ is sliding down a snowy slope at a constant acceleration. Find the coefficient of kinetic friction for the skier if friction is known to be $45.0\\text{ N}$. "
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Strategy"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "The magnitude of kinetic friction is given as $45.0\\text{ N}$. Kinetic friction is related to the normal force by $f_k = \\mu_k N$; thus, we can find the coefficient of kinetic friction if we can find the normal force on the skier. The normal force is always perpendicular to the surface, and since there is no motion perpendicular to the surface, the normal force should equal the component of the skier's weight perpendicular to the slope. (See Figure 6.14, which repeats a figure from the chapter on Newton's laws of motion.)"
    },
    {
      "type": "figure",
      "text": "FIGURE 6.14 The motion of the skier and friction are parallel to the slope, so it is most convenient to project all forces onto a coordinate system where one axis is parallel to the slope and the other is perpendicular (axes shown to left of skier). The normal force is perpendicular to the slope, and friction is parallel to the slope, but the skier's weight has components along both axes, namely $\\vec{w}_y$ and $\\vec{w}_x$. The normal force $\\vec{N}$ is equal in magnitude to $\\vec{w}_y$, so there is no motion perpendicular to the slope. — A skier sliding down a $25^{\\circ}$ snowy inclined plane. A coordinate system is shown to the left with $y$ perpendicular to the slope and $x$ parallel down the slope. Forces acting on the skier include normal force $\\vec{N}$ perpendicular up from the surface, kinetic friction $\\vec{f}$ pointing up the slope, weight $\\vec{w}$ pointing straight down, and components of weight $\\vec{w}_y$ pointing straight down perpendicular to the slope and $\\vec{w}_x$ pointing down the slope. A separate free-body diagram on the right shows these same force vectors originating from a point mass representing the skier, with the $25^{\\circ}$ angle indicated between the vertical and the $y$-axis.",
      "label": "Figure 6.14",
      "box": [
        542,
        155,
        815,
        843
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "We have"
    }
