    {
      "type": "equation",
      "text": "\\frac{g - (bv/m)}{g} = e^{-bt/m}"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "and"
    },
    {
      "type": "equation",
      "text": "v = \\frac{mg}{b}(1 - e^{-bt/m})."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Notice that as $t \\to \\infty$, $v \\to mg/b = v_T$, which is the terminal velocity."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "The position at any time may be found by integrating the equation for v. With $v = dy/dt$,"
    },
    {
      "type": "equation",
      "text": "dy = \\frac{mg}{b}(1 - e^{-bt/m}) dt."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Assuming $y = 0$ when $t = 0$,"
    },
    {
      "type": "equation",
      "text": "\\int_0^y dy' = \\frac{mg}{b} \\int_0^t (1 - e^{-bt'/m}) dt',"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "which integrates to"
    },
    {
      "type": "equation",
      "text": "y = \\frac{mg}{b}t + \\frac{m^2g}{b^2}(e^{-bt/m} - 1)."
    },
    {
      "type": "example",
      "text": "EXAMPLE 6.18\nEffect of the Resistive Force on a Motorboat\nA motorboat is moving across a lake at a speed $v_0$ when its motor suddenly freezes up and stops. The boat then slows down under the frictional force $f_R = -bv$. (a) What are the velocity and position of the boat as functions of time? (b) If the boat slows down from 4.0 to 1.0 m/s in 10 s, how far does it travel before stopping?\nSolution\na. With the motor stopped, the only horizontal force on the boat is $f_R = -bv$, so from Newton's second law,\nm\\frac{dv}{dt} = -bv,\nwhich we can write as\n\\frac{dv}{v} = -\\frac{b}{m}dt.\nIntegrating this equation between the time zero when the velocity is $v_0$ and the time $t$ when the velocity is $v$, we have\n\\int_{v_0}^v \\frac{dv'}{v'} = -\\frac{b}{m} \\int_0^t dt'.\nThus,\n\\ln \\frac{v}{v_0} = -\\frac{b}{m}t,\nwhich, since $\\ln A = x$ implies $e^x = A$, we can write this as\nv = v_0 e^{-bt/m}.\nNow from the definition of velocity,\n\\frac{dx}{dt} = v_0 e^{-bt/m},\nso we have\ndx = v_0 e^{-bt/m} dt.\nWith the initial position zero, we have"
