{
  "blocks": [
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "force is generally a complicated function of the body’s velocity. However, for a body moving in a straight line at moderate speeds through a liquid such as water, the frictional force can often be approximated by"
    },
    {
      "type": "equation",
      "text": "f_R = -bv,"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "where b is a constant whose value depends on the dimensions and shape of the body and the properties of the liquid, and v is the velocity of the body. Two situations for which the frictional force can be represented by this equation are a motorboat moving through water and a small object falling slowly through a liquid."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Let’s consider the object falling through a liquid. The free-body diagram of this object with the positive direction downward is shown in Figure 6.33. Newton’s second law in the vertical direction gives the differential equation"
    },
    {
      "type": "equation",
      "text": "mg - bv = m \\frac{dv}{dt},"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "where we have written the acceleration as dv/dt. As v increases, the frictional force -bv increases until it matches mg. At this point, there is no acceleration and the velocity remains constant at the terminal velocity v_T. From the previous equation,"
    },
    {
      "type": "equation",
      "text": "mg - bv_T = 0,"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "so"
    },
    {
      "type": "equation",
      "text": "v_T = \\frac{mg}{b}."
    },
    {
      "type": "figure",
      "text": "FIGURE 6.33 Free-body diagram of an object falling through a resistive medium. — A free-body diagram showing a blue sphere falling vertically. Two red vectors originate from the center of the sphere: one pointing upward labeled bv, and one pointing downward labeled mg. A vertical axis labeled y points downward next to the sphere, and a small vector labeled v points downward next to the sphere.",
      "label": "6.33",
      "box": [
        397,
        328,
        595,
        671
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "We can find the object’s velocity by integrating the differential equation for v. First, we rearrange terms in this equation to obtain"
    },
    {
      "type": "equation",
      "text": "\\frac{dv}{g - (b/m)v} = dt."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Assuming that v = 0 at t = 0, integration of this equation yields"
    },
    {
      "type": "equation",
      "text": "\\int_{0}^{v} \\frac{dv'}{g - (b/m)v'} = \\int_{0}^{t} dt',"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "or"
    },
    {
      "type": "equation",
      "text": "-\\frac{m}{b} \\ln \\left( g - \\frac{b}{m}v \\right) \\Big|_0^v = t' \\Big|_0^t,"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "where v' and t' are dummy variables of integration. With the limits given, we find"
    },
    {
      "type": "equation",
      "text": "-\\frac{m}{b} \\left[ \\ln \\left( g - \\frac{b}{m}v \\right) - \\ln g \\right] = t."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Since ln A - ln B = ln(A/B), and ln(A/B) = x implies e^x = A/B, we obtain"
    }
  ]
}

