      "type": "equation",
      "text": "\\int_{0}^{x} dx' = v_0 \\int_{0}^{t} e^{-bt'/m} dt'"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "and"
    },
    {
      "type": "equation",
      "text": "x = -\\frac{mv_0}{b} e^{-bt'/m} \\Big|_0^t = \\frac{mv_0}{b}(1 - e^{-bt/m})."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "As time increases, $e^{-bt/m} \\to 0$, and the position of the boat approaches a limiting value"
    },
    {
      "type": "equation",
      "text": "x_{\\text{max}} = \\frac{mv_0}{b}."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Although this tells us that the boat takes an infinite amount of time to reach $x_{\\text{max}}$, the boat effectively stops after a reasonable time. For example, at $t = 10m/b$, we have"
    },
    {
      "type": "equation",
      "text": "v = v_0 e^{-10} \\simeq 4.5 \\times 10^{-5} v_0,"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "whereas we also have"
    },
    {
      "type": "equation",
      "text": "x = x_{\\text{max}}(1 - e^{-10}) \\simeq 0.99995 x_{\\text{max}}."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Therefore, the boat’s velocity and position have essentially reached their final values."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "b. With $v_0 = 4.0 \\text{ m/s}$ and $v = 1.0 \\text{ m/s}$, we have $1.0 \\text{ m/s} = (4.0 \\text{ m/s})e^{-(b/m)(10 \\text{ s})}$, so"
    },
    {
      "type": "equation",
      "text": "\\ln 0.25 = -\\ln 4.0 = -\\frac{b}{m}(10 \\text{ s}),"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "and"
    },
    {
      "type": "equation",
      "text": "\\frac{b}{m} = \\frac{1}{10} \\ln 4.0 \\text{ s}^{-1} = 0.14 \\text{ s}^{-1}."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Now the boat’s limiting position is"
    },
    {
      "type": "equation",
      "text": "x_{\\text{max}} = \\frac{mv_0}{b} = \\frac{4.0 \\text{ m/s}}{0.14 \\text{ s}^{-1}} = 29 \\text{ m}."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Significance"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "In the both of the previous examples, we found “limiting” values. The terminal velocity is the same as the limiting velocity, which is the velocity of the falling object after a (relatively) long time has passed. Similarly, the limiting distance of the boat is the distance the boat will travel after a long amount of time has passed. Due to the properties of exponential decay, the time involved to reach either of these values is actually not too long (certainly not an infinite amount of time!) but they are quickly found by taking the limit to infinity."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "CHECK YOUR UNDERSTANDING 6.11"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Suppose the resistive force of the air on a skydiver can be approximated by $f = -bv^2$. If the terminal velocity of a 100-kg skydiver is 60 m/s, what is the value of b?"
    }
  ]
}

